    "\n    in `std::text`\n    removed: write &v[lo..hi] (D-0308)\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::io\u{1f}stdout_write\u{1f}extern fn\u{1f}export u"
    "nsafe extern fn stdout_write(rawptr<u8> buf, usize len) : isize\u{1f}Writes the `len` bytes at `buf` to s"
    "tandard output: the primitive under `printf`.\u{1f}\u{1f}export unsafe extern fn stdout_write(rawptr<u8> buf, "
    "usize len) : isize\n    in `std::io`\n\n    Writes the `len` bytes at `buf` to standard output: the pri"
    "mitive under `printf`.\n\n    Called inside `unsafe`, with `buf` pointing at `len` readable bytes; its"
    " result is a claim like any\n    extern's. What it writes goes through the same buffer as `printf` (`"
    "flush_stdout`). Programs write with\n    `printf`.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::io\u{1f}stdin_read\u{1f}extern fn\u{1f}export unsafe exter"
    "n fn stdin_read(rawptr<u8> buf, usize len) : isize\u{1f}Reads up to `len` bytes of standard input into `b"
    "uf`: the primitive under `read_all_bytes`.\u{1f}\u{1f}export unsafe extern fn stdin_read(rawptr<u8> buf, usize"
    " len) : isize\n    in `std::io`\n\n    Reads up to `len` bytes of standard input into `buf`: the primit"
    "ive under `read_all_bytes`.\n\n    Called inside `unsafe`, with `buf` pointing at `len` writable bytes"
    ". Its result, a claim like any\n    extern's, is the number of bytes read: 0 at the end of the input,"
    " negative when the input cannot be read.\n    Programs read with `read_line`, `read_all` or `read_all"
    "_bytes`.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::io\u{1f}flush_stdout\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn flush_stdout()\u{1f}Writes out what standard output still ho"
    "lds in its buffer.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn flush_stdout()\n    in `std::io`\n\n    Writes out what standard output s"
    "till holds in its buffer.\n\n    Standard output is buffered; standard error is not. Reading standard "
    "input, writing to standard error,\n    `exit` and the program's end write the buffer out themselves, "
    "so this is needed only when standard output\n    is a pipe or a file that someone watches while the p"
    "rogram runs (progress, a log), and before handing over\n    to C code that writes through `stdio`.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}"
    "std::io\u{1f}read_line\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn read_line() : Result<Option<String>, FileError>\u{1f}The next line of stan"
    "dard input, without its line ending, or `Ok(None)` at the end of the input.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn read_line() :"
    " Result<Option<String>, FileError>\n    in `std::io`\n\n    The next line of standard input, without it"
    "s line ending, or `Ok(None)` at the end of the input.\n\n    - A line is every byte up to the next `\\n"
    "`, which is consumed and not included; a `\\r` just before it is\n      consumed with it, so a Windows"
    " line ending reads as a Unix one. A last line with no `\\n` is still a\n      line, and keeps a final "
    "`\\r`.\n    - `Ok(None)` when the input ended before the line's first byte, and on every call after th"
    "at.\n    - `Err(FileError::Utf8(e))` when the line is not UTF-8, `e.offset` counted within the line; "
    "the line is\n      consumed, so the next call reads the one after it.\n    - `Err(FileError::Io)` when"
    " standard input cannot be read; the bytes of the line read so far are lost.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::io\u{1f}read_all_bytes"
    "\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn read_all_bytes() : Result<Vec<u8>, FileError>\u{1f}The rest of standard input, as bytes; em"
    "pty when the input has already ended.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn read_all_bytes() : Result<Vec<u8>, FileError>\n    i"
    "n `std::io`\n\n    The rest of standard input, as bytes; empty when the input has already ended.\n\n    "
    "`Err(FileError::Io)` when standard input cannot be read. `read_all` takes it as text.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::io\u{1f}read"
    "_all\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn read_all() : Result<String, FileError>\u{1f}The rest of standard input, as text.\u{1f}\u{1f}expor"
    "t fn read_all() : Result<String, FileError>\n    in `std::io`\n\n    The rest of standard input, as tex"
    "t.\n\n    `Err(FileError::Utf8(e))` when it is not UTF-8, and `Err(FileError::Io)` when it cannot be r"
    "ead;\n    `read_all_bytes` takes it as bytes.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}FileError\u{1f}enum\u{1f}export enum FileError\u{1f}The erro"
    "r of every input and output operation of `std`, for files and standard input alike.\u{1f}\u{1f}export enum Fil"
    "eError\n    in `std::fs`\n\n    The error of every input and output operation of `std`, for files and s"
    "tandard input alike.\n\n    - `NotFound`: no such file, or a directory on the path does not exist.\n   "
    " - `Denied`: the environment refuses the operation.\n    - `IsDirectory`: the path names a directory "
    "where a file is needed.\n    - `NotDirectory`: a part of the path that must be a directory is not one"
    ".\n    - `NoSpace`: the device (or the user's quota on it) is full.\n    - `TooManyOpen`: the program,"
    " or the system, has as many files open as it may.\n    - `Io`: any other failure (a device error, …"
    ").\n    - `Utf8(e)`: the bytes read are not UTF-8; `e` gives the offset of the first byte that is not"
    ".\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}FileError::text\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn FileError::text(ref<FileError, shared> e) : str\u{1f}The error a"
    "s a short phrase for a person (`not found`, `permission denied`).\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn FileError::text(ref<Fil"
    "eError, shared> e) : str\n    in `std::fs`\n\n    The error as a short phrase for a person (`not found`"
    ", `permission denied`).\n\n    Example, \"FileError::text: a phrase to print\":\n        FileError e = Fi"
    "leError::NotFound;\n        assert(FileError::text(&e) == \"not found\");\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}read_file\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export"
    " fn read_file(StringView path) : Result<String, FileError>\u{1f}The whole contents of the file at `path`,"
    " as text.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn read_file(StringView path) : Result<String, FileError>\n    in `std::fs`\n\n    Th"
    "e whole contents of the file at `path`, as text.\n\n    A relative path is resolved against the workin"
    "g directory. `Err(Utf8(e))` when the bytes are not UTF-8,\n    `e` giving the offset of the first tha"
    "t is not; `Err(NotFound)` when no such file exists; `Err(Denied)`\n    when the environment refuses; "
    "`Err(IsDirectory)` for a directory; `Err(Io)` for another failure. Use\n    `read_bytes` for bytes th"
    "at need not be text, and `File` for a file too large to hold.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}write_file\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn writ"
    "e_file(StringView path, StringView text) : Result<void, FileError>\u{1f}Makes the file at `path` hold exa"
    "ctly `text`, creating it or replacing its contents.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn write_file(StringView path, StringVie"
    "w text) : Result<void, FileError>\n    in `std::fs`\n\n    Makes the file at `path` hold exactly `text`"
    ", creating it or replacing its contents.\n\n    `Err(NotFound)` when a directory on the path does not "
    "exist; `Err(Denied)` when the environment refuses\n    the write; `Err(Io)` for any other failure.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}"
    "std::fs\u{1f}File\u{1f}struct\u{1f}export resource struct File\u{1f}An open file, read and written in order from a posit"
    "ion that `seek` moves.\u{1f}\u{1f}export resource struct File\n    in `std::fs`\n\n    An open file, read and wri"
    "tten in order from a position that `seek` moves.\n\n    Every `File` comes from `File::open`, `create`"
    ", `append`, `open_rw` or `create_new`. Positions are `u64`,\n    so a file larger than the address sp"
    "ace is still addressed. Reading and writing are buffered; the bytes\n    written reach the file no la"
    "ter than the next operation on it, any other file-system operation of the\n    program, the start of "
    "a child process, or the program's end.\n\n    Destroying a `File` closes it without making it durable "
    "and without reporting a failure; a program\n    that must know its bytes were stored calls `File::clo"
    "se`.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}File::open\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn File::open(StringView path) : Result<File, FileError>\u{1f}Opens t"
    "he existing file at `path` for reading, at position 0.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn File::open(StringView path) : Resu"
    "lt<File, FileError>\n    in `std::fs`\n\n    Opens the existing file at `path` for reading, at position"
    " 0.\n\n    `Err(NotFound)` when there is no such file; `Err(Denied)` when the environment refuses; `Er"
    "r(IsDirectory)`\n    when the path names a directory; the other variants of `FileError` for the failu"
    "res they name.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}File::create\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn File::create(StringView path) : Result<File, File"
    "Error>\u{1f}Opens the file at `path` for writing from empty, creating it or discarding its contents.\u{1f}\u{1f}exp"
    "ort fn File::create(StringView path) : Result<File, FileError>\n    in `std::fs`\n\n    Opens the file "
    "at `path` for writing from empty, creating it or discarding its contents.\n\n    `Err(NotFound)` when "
    "a directory on the path does not exist; `Err(Denied)`, `Err(Io)` as `File::open`'s.\n    Use `File::c"
    "reate_new` when an existing file must not be overwritten.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}File::append\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn File::"
    "append(StringView path) : Result<File, FileError>\u{1f}Opens the file at `path` for writing at its end, c"
    "reating it if it does not exist.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn File::append(StringView path) : Result<File, FileError>\n"
    "    in `std::fs`\n\n    Opens the file at `path` for writing at its end, creating it if it does not ex"
    "ist.\n\n    Every write lands at the end of the file.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}File::open_rw\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn File::open_"
    "rw(StringView path) : Result<File, FileError>\u{1f}Opens the file at `path` for reading and writing, crea"
    "ting it if it does not exist, its contents kept.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn File::open_rw(StringView path) : Result<"
    "File, FileError>\n    in `std::fs`\n\n    Opens the file at `path` for reading and writing, creating it"
    " if it does not exist, its contents kept.\n\n    The position starts at 0; a write lands at the positi"
    "on reading has reached.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}File::read\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn File::read(ref<File, exclusive> f, ref<Vec"
    "<u8>, exclusive> buf, usize max) : Result<usize, FileError>\u{1f}Appends up to `max` bytes from the file'"
    "s position to the end of `buf` and returns how many.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn File::read(ref<File, exclusive> f, r"
    "ef<Vec<u8>, exclusive> buf, usize max) : Result<usize, FileError>\n    in `std::fs`\n\n    Appends up t"
    "o `max` bytes from the file's position to the end of `buf` and returns how many.\n\n    At most a MiB "
    "is read at a time, whatever `max` is; fewer bytes come only at the end of the file, and\n    `Ok(0)` "
    "(for a `max` above 0) means the end. `Err(Io)` on a file opened only for writing.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}File::re"
    "ad_to_end\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn File::read_to_end(ref<File, exclusive> f, ref<Vec<u8>, exclusive> buf) : Resu"
    "lt<usize, FileError>\u{1f}Appends the rest of the file, from its position, to the end of `buf` and return"
    "s how many bytes.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn File::read_to_end(ref<File, exclusive> f, ref<Vec<u8>, exclusive> buf) "
    ": Result<usize, FileError>\n    in `std::fs`\n\n    Appends the rest of the file, from its position, to"
    " the end of `buf` and returns how many bytes.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}File::read_line\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn File::read_line"
    "(ref<File, exclusive> f) : Result<Option<String>, FileError>\u{1f}The next line of the file, without its "
    "final `'\\n'` or one `'\\r'` just before it.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn File::read_line(ref<File, exclusive> f) : Resu"
    "lt<Option<String>, FileError>\n    in `std::fs`\n\n    The next line of the file, without its final `'\\"
    "n'` or one `'\\r'` just before it.\n\n    `Ok(None)` at the end of the file. A last line with no `'\\n'`"
    " is still a line. `Err(Utf8(e))` when the\n    line is not UTF-8, `e`'s offset counted from the line'"
    "s start; `Err(Io)` on a file opened only for\n    writing. Lines end as `read_line` on standard input"
    " ends them.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}File::write\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn File::write(ref<File, exclusive> f, slice<u8, shared>"
    " data) : Result<void, FileError>\u{1f}Writes all of `data` at the file's position, or at its end for a fi"
    "le opened by `File::append`.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn File::write(ref<File, exclusive> f, slice<u8, shared> data) "
    ": Result<void, FileError>\n    in `std::fs`\n\n    Writes all of `data` at the file's position, or at i"
    "ts end for a file opened by `File::append`.\n\n    The file grows as needed and the position moves pas"
    "t the bytes written. Writes are buffered (see `File`);\n    a failure the environment reports late is"
    " reported by the file's next operation. `Err(Io)` on a file\n    opened only for reading.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}F"
    "ile::write_text\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn File::write_text(ref<File, exclusive> f, StringView text) : Result<void"
    ", FileError>\u{1f}Writes the bytes of `text` at the file's position, as `File::write` does.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Fi"
    "le::write_text(ref<File, exclusive> f, StringView text) : Result<void, FileError>\n    in `std::fs`\n\n"
    "    Writes the bytes of `text` at the file's position, as `File::write` does.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}File::seek\u{1f}f"
    "n\u{1f}export fn File::seek(ref<File, exclusive> f, u64 pos) : Result<void, FileError>\u{1f}Moves the file's p"
    "osition to byte `pos` from the start.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn File::seek(ref<File, exclusive> f, u64 pos) : Resul"
    "t<void, FileError>\n    in `std::fs`\n\n    Moves the file's position to byte `pos` from the start.\n\n  "
    "  A position beyond the end is allowed: reading there gives 0 bytes, and writing there extends the f"
    "ile.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}File::len\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn File::len(ref<File, shared> f) : Result<u64, FileError>\u{1f}The fi"
    "le's length in bytes.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn File::len(ref<File, shared> f) : Result<u64, FileError>\n    in `std"
    "::fs`\n\n    The file's length in bytes.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}File::create_new\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn File::create_new(Stri"
    "ngView path) : Result<Option<File>, FileError>\u{1f}Opens a new file at `path` for writing, only if nothi"
    "ng is there yet.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn File::create_new(StringView path) : Result<Option<File>, FileError>\n    "
    "in `std::fs`\n\n    Opens a new file at `path` for writing, only if nothing is there yet.\n\n    `Ok(Non"
    "e)` when a file or a directory of that name already exists, and nothing is changed, so of two\n    pr"
    "ograms that race to make the same file exactly one wins: a lock file, a pid file, a file that must\n "
    "   never be overwritten. `Err(NotFound)` when a directory above `path` does not exist; other errors "
    "as\n    `File::create`'s.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}File::flush\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn File::flush(ref<File, exclusive> f) : Re"
    "sult<void, FileError>\u{1f}Makes everything written to the file so far durable.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn File::flush(re"
    "f<File, exclusive> f) : Result<void, FileError>\n    in `std::fs`\n\n    Makes everything written to th"
    "e file so far durable.\n\n    The bytes are handed to the operating system and by it to the device, an"
    "d from then on survive a crash\n    or a power loss; the write-then-rename pattern flushes before the"
    " rename. `Err(Io)` on a file open only\n    for reading, or when the device fails.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}File::po"
    "sition\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn File::position(ref<File, shared> f) : Result<u64, FileError>\u{1f}The byte the next r"
    "ead or write starts at, counted from the start of the file.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn File::position(ref<File, shar"
    "ed> f) : Result<u64, FileError>\n    in `std::fs`\n\n    The byte the next read or write starts at, cou"
    "nted from the start of the file.\n\n    It is 0 when the file is opened (for `File::append`, the lengt"
    "h once something is written) and is moved\n    by every read, `read_line`, write and `seek`.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::f"
    "s\u{1f}File::close\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn File::close(File f) : Result<void, FileError>\u{1f}Closes the file, first maki"
    "ng a file open for writing durable, and reports any failure.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn File::close(File f) : Result"
    "<void, FileError>\n    in `std::fs`\n\n    Closes the file, first making a file open for writing durabl"
    "e, and reports any failure.\n\n    `Err(e)` when the environment reports a failure, including one for "
    "bytes written earlier; the file is\n    closed all the same. Destroying a `File` closes it too, but r"
    "eports nothing.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}File::drop\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn File::drop(ref<File, exclusive> self)\u{1f}Closes an op"
    "en file when its `File` is destroyed, without making it durable or reporting a failure.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn F"
    "ile::drop(ref<File, exclusive> self)\n    in `std::fs`\n\n    Closes an open file when its `File` is de"
    "stroyed, without making it durable or reporting a failure.\n\n    The destructor; programs do not call"
    " it, and call `File::close` to learn whether the bytes were stored.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}read_bytes\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export f"
    "n read_bytes(StringView path) : Result<Vec<u8>, FileError>\u{1f}The whole contents of the file at `path`,"
    " as bytes.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn read_bytes(StringView path) : Result<Vec<u8>, FileError>\n    in `std::fs`\n\n   "
    " The whole contents of the file at `path`, as bytes.\n\n    The errors are `File::open`'s and `File::r"
    "ead_to_end`'s. Use `read_file` for text.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}write_bytes\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn write_bytes(StringView p"
    "ath, slice<u8, shared> data) : Result<void, FileError>\u{1f}Makes the file at `path` hold exactly `data`,"
    " creating it or replacing its contents.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn write_bytes(StringView path, slice<u8, shared> da"
    "ta) : Result<void, FileError>\n    in `std::fs`\n\n    Makes the file at `path` hold exactly `data`, cr"
    "eating it or replacing its contents.\n\n    The errors are `File::create`'s and `File::write`'s. Use `"
    "write_file` for text.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}PathKind\u{1f}enum\u{1f}export enum PathKind\u{1f}What a path names, as `path_kind`"
    " and `file_info` report it.\u{1f}\u{1f}export enum PathKind\n    in `std::fs`\n\n    What a path names, as `path_"
    "kind` and `file_info` report it.\n\n    - `File(n)`: a file of `n` bytes.\n    - `Dir`: a directory.\n  "
    "  - `Other`: something else, such as a device or a socket.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}make_dir\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn make_dir("
    "StringView path) : Result<bool, FileError>\u{1f}Makes the directory `path`, whose parent must exist.\u{1f}\u{1f}exp"
    "ort fn make_dir(StringView path) : Result<bool, FileError>\n    in `std::fs`\n\n    Makes the directory"
    " `path`, whose parent must exist.\n\n    `Ok(true)` when it was made; `Ok(false)` when a directory was"
    " already there, so it is safe to call\n    again. `Err(NotFound)` when the parent does not exist; `Er"
    "r(Io)` when something that is not a directory\n    is at `path`. Use `make_dir_all` to make the missi"
    "ng parents too.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}remove_file\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn remove_file(StringView path) : Result<void, FileE"
    "rror>\u{1f}Removes the file at `path`.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn remove_file(StringView path) : Result<void, FileError>\n"
    "    in `std::fs`\n\n    Removes the file at `path`.\n\n    `Err(NotFound)` when there is none.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs"
    "\u{1f}remove_dir\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn remove_dir(StringView path) : Result<void, FileError>\u{1f}Removes the empty dir"
    "ectory at `path`.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn remove_dir(StringView path) : Result<void, FileError>\n    in `std::fs`\n"
    "\n    Removes the empty directory at `path`.\n\n    `Err(Io)` when it is not empty; `Err(NotFound)` whe"
    "n there is none. Use `remove_dir_all` to remove a\n    directory and everything in it.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}rena"
    "me\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn rename(StringView from, StringView to) : Result<void, FileError>\u{1f}Renames (moves) wha"
    "t is at `from` to `to`, replacing a file at `to`.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn rename(StringView from, StringView to) "
    ": Result<void, FileError>\n    in `std::fs`\n\n    Renames (moves) what is at `from` to `to`, replacing"
    " a file at `to`.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}list_dir\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn list_dir(StringView path) : Result<Vec<String>, Fil"
    "eError>\u{1f}The names of the entries of the directory `path`, sorted by their bytes.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn list_dir"
    "(StringView path) : Result<Vec<String>, FileError>\n    in `std::fs`\n\n    The names of the entries of"
    " the directory `path`, sorted by their bytes.\n\n    The names are entry names, not paths (join them t"
    "o `path` with `path_join`), and never `.` or `..`.\n    `Err(Utf8(e))` for a name that is not UTF-8.\n"
    "\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}path_kind\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn path_kind(StringView path) : Result<PathKind, FileError>\u{1f}What `path"
    "` names: a file and its length, a directory, or something else.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn path_kind(StringView path"
    ") : Result<PathKind, FileError>\n    in `std::fs`\n\n    What `path` names: a file and its length, a di"
    "rectory, or something else.\n\n    A symbolic link is followed. `Err(NotFound)` when nothing is at `pa"
    "th`.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}path_join\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn path_join(StringView base, StringView tail) : String\u{1f}The path "
    "`base` followed by `tail`, with the platform's separator between them.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn path_join(StringVi"
    "ew base, StringView tail) : String\n    in `std::fs`\n\n    The path `base` followed by `tail`, with th"
    "e platform's separator between them.\n\n    No separator is added after one `base` already ends with. "
    "An absolute `tail` is returned alone. Reads the\n    text only, never the file system.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}path"
    "_parent\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn path_parent(StringView p) : Option<String>\u{1f}The path `p` without its last part.\u{1f}"
    "\u{1f}export fn path_parent(StringView p) : Option<String>\n    in `std::fs`\n\n    The path `p` without its"
    " last part.\n\n    `None` for a root or the empty path; the empty path for a single relative name. Rea"
    "ds the text only,\n    never the file system; the grammar is the platform's (`\\` also separates parts"
    " on Windows).\n\n    Example, \"path_parent: a single name\":\n        assert(Option::unwrap(path_parent("
    "\"notes.txt\")) == \"\");\n        assert(Option::is_none(&path_parent(\"\")));\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}path_file_name\u{1f}fn"
    "\u{1f}export fn path_file_name(StringView p) : Option<String>\u{1f}The last part of the path `p`.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn p"
    "ath_file_name(StringView p) : Option<String>\n    in `std::fs`\n\n    The last part of the path `p`.\n\n "
    "   `None` when the last part is `..`, or when there is none (`/`, the empty path). Reads the text on"
    "ly.\n\n    Example, \"path_file_name: a final ..\":\n        assert(Option::unwrap(path_file_name(\"src/ma"
    "in.cb\")) == \"main.cb\");\n        assert(Option::is_none(&path_file_name(\"src/..\")));\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}path_s"
    "tem\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn path_stem(StringView p) : Option<String>\u{1f}The file name of `p` without its extension"
    " (`archive.tar` of `archive.tar.gz`).\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn path_stem(StringView p) : Option<String>\n    in `st"
    "d::fs`\n\n    The file name of `p` without its extension (`archive.tar` of `archive.tar.gz`).\n\n    A n"
    "ame whose only dot is its first byte has no extension, so its stem is the whole name (`.bashrc`).\n  "
    "  Reads the text only.\n\n    Example, \"path_stem: the last dot\":\n        assert(Option::unwrap(path_s"
    "tem(\"archive.tar.gz\")) == \"archive.tar\");\n        assert(Option::unwrap(path_stem(\".bashrc\")) == \".b"
    "ashrc\");\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}path_extension\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn path_extension(StringView p) : Option<String>\u{1f}The ext"
    "ension of the file name of `p`, without its dot (`gz` of `archive.tar.gz`).\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn path_extensio"
    "n(StringView p) : Option<String>\n    in `std::fs`\n\n    The extension of the file name of `p`, withou"
    "t its dot (`gz` of `archive.tar.gz`).\n\n    `None` when there is none: a name with no dot, or whose o"
    "nly dot is its first byte. Reads the text only.\n\n    Example, \"path_extension: no extension\":\n      "
    "  assert(Option::unwrap(path_extension(\"archive.tar.gz\")) == \"gz\");\n        assert(Option::is_none(&"
    "path_extension(\".bashrc\")));\n        assert(Option::is_none(&path_extension(\"README\")));\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}p"
    "ath_is_absolute\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn path_is_absolute(StringView p) : bool\u{1f}Whether `p` names a place without"
    " the working directory.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn path_is_absolute(StringView p) : bool\n    in `std::fs`\n\n    Wheth"
    "er `p` names a place without the working directory.\n\n    By the platform's grammar: a leading `/` on"
    " Unix; `C:\\` or `\\\\server\\share\\` on Windows. Reads the text\n    only.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}path_normalize\u{1f}fn\u{1f}e"
    "xport fn path_normalize(StringView p) : String\u{1f}The path `p` with every `.` part removed and every `x"
    "/..` (`x` a name) resolved.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn path_normalize(StringView p) : String\n    in `std::fs`\n\n    T"
    "he path `p` with every `.` part removed and every `x/..` (`x` a name) resolved.\n\n    By the text alo"
    "ne: a link is not followed, so the result can name another place than `p` when `x` is a\n    link. A "
    "leading `..` stays, `/..` is `/`, and a path with no part left is `.`. Use `path_canonical` to\n    a"
    "sk the file system.\n\n    Example, \"path_normalize: . and ..\":\n        assert(path_normalize(\"a/./b/."
    "./c\") == path_join(\"a\", \"c\"));\n        assert(path_normalize(\"../x/..\") == \"..\");\n        assert(pat"
    "h_normalize(\"a/..\") == \".\");\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}path_canonical\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn path_canonical(StringView p) : Re"
    "sult<String, FileError>\u{1f}The absolute path the operating system resolves `p` to, with every link foll"
    "owed.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn path_canonical(StringView p) : Result<String, FileError>\n    in `std::fs`\n\n    The "
    "absolute path the operating system resolves `p` to, with every link followed.\n\n    `p` must exist: `"
    "Err(NotFound)` when nothing is at `p`; `Err(Utf8(e))` when the result is not UTF-8.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}FileIn"
    "fo\u{1f}struct\u{1f}export struct FileInfo\u{1f}What `file_info` reports of a path.\u{1f}\u{1f}export struct FileInfo\n    in "
    "`std::fs`\n\n    What `file_info` reports of a path.\n\n    - `kind`: what the path names, as `path_kind"
    "` gives it.\n    - `len`: the length in bytes.\n    - `modified_unix`: the last modification, in whole"
    " seconds since 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z (rounded down).\n    - `readonly`: whether the file is read-only."
    "\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}file_info\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn file_info(StringView path) : Result<FileInfo, FileError>\u{1f}The kind,"
    " length, modification time and read-only state of what is at `path`.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn file_info(StringView"
    " path) : Result<FileInfo, FileError>\n    in `std::fs`\n\n    The kind, length, modification time and r"
    "ead-only state of what is at `path`.\n\n    A symbolic link is followed, as `path_kind` follows it. `E"
    "rr(NotFound)` when nothing is at `path`.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}copy_file\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn copy_file(StringView from,"
    " StringView to) : Result<u64, FileError>\u{1f}Copies the file `from` to `to`, replacing a file there, and"
    " returns the number of bytes copied.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn copy_file(StringView from, StringView to) : Result<u"
    "64, FileError>\n    in `std::fs`\n\n    Copies the file `from` to `to`, replacing a file there, and ret"
    "urns the number of bytes copied.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}copy_permissions\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn copy_permissions(StringView"
    " from, StringView to) : Result<void, FileError>\u{1f}Gives what is at `to` the permissions of what is at "
    "`from`: on Unix the mode bits, on Windows the read-only flag.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn copy_permissions(StringView"
    " from, StringView to) : Result<void, FileError>\n    in `std::fs`\n\n    Gives what is at `to` the perm"
    "issions of what is at `from`: on Unix the mode bits, on Windows the\n    read-only flag.\n\n    For the"
    " write-then-rename pattern: a file written beside the original and renamed over it keeps the\n    ori"
    "ginal's permissions only when they are copied to it before the rename. `Err(NotFound)` when nothing\n"
    "    is at `from` or at `to`.\n\n    Example, \"copy_permissions: one file's permissions given to anothe"
    "r\":\n        String tmp = temp_dir();\n        String dir = path_join(&tmp, &sprintf(\"cb-perm-%v\", pro"
    "cess_id()));\n        _ = make_dir_all(&dir);\n        String a = path_join(&dir, \"a.txt\");\n        St"
    "ring b = path_join(&dir, \"b.txt\");\n        _ = write_file(&a, \"a\");\n        _ = write_file(&b, \"b\");"
    "\n        assert(Result::is_ok(&copy_permissions(&a, &b)));\n        bool ra = Result::unwrap(file_inf"
    "o(&a)).readonly;\n        bool rb = Result::unwrap(file_info(&b)).readonly;\n        assert(ra == rb);"
    "\n        String gone = path_join(&dir, \"gone.txt\");\n        assert(Result::is_err(&copy_permissions("
    "&gone, &b)));\n        _ = remove_dir_all(&dir);\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}make_dir_all\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn make_dir_all(Str"
    "ingView path) : Result<void, FileError>\u{1f}Makes the directory `path` and every missing directory above"
    " it.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn make_dir_all(StringView path) : Result<void, FileError>\n    in `std::fs`\n\n    Makes "
    "the directory `path` and every missing directory above it.\n\n    `Ok(())` when they are all there alr"
    "eady.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::fs\u{1f}remove_dir_all\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn remove_dir_all(StringView path) : Result<void, FileError"
    ">\u{1f}Removes the directory `path` and everything in it.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn remove_dir_all(StringView path) : Re"
    "sult<void, FileError>\n    in `std::fs`\n\n    Removes the directory `path` and everything in it.\n\n    "
    "`Err(NotFound)` when nothing is at `path`.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::env\u{1f}arg_bytes\u{1f}extern fn\u{1f}export unsafe extern fn ar"
    "g_bytes(usize i, rawptr<u8> buf, usize len) : isize\u{1f}Copies at most `len` bytes of argument `i` to `b"
    "uf` and returns the argument's whole length.\u{1f}\u{1f}export unsafe extern fn arg_bytes(usize i, rawptr<u8> "
    "buf, usize len) : isize\n    in `std::env`\n\n    Copies at most `len` bytes of argument `i` to `buf` a"
    "nd returns the argument's whole length.\n\n    Returns -1, storing nothing, when there is no argument "
    "`i`; a call with `len` 0 only asks whether\n    argument `i` exists and how long it is. Called inside"
    " `unsafe`: `[buf, buf + len)` must be storage\n    outside every live object. For an argument's bytes"
    " whatever they are; `arg` is the safe way to read one.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::env\u{1f}arg_count\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn arg_count()"
    " : usize\u{1f}The number of the program's arguments, not counting the program's own name.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn arg_"
    "count() : usize\n    in `std::env`\n\n    The number of the program's arguments, not counting the progr"
    "am's own name.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::env\u{1f}arg\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn arg(usize i) : Result<String, Utf8Error>\u{1f}Argument `i` of "
    "the program, counted from 0 for the first after the program's own name.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn arg(usize i) : Re"
    "sult<String, Utf8Error>\n    in `std::env`\n\n    Argument `i` of the program, counted from 0 for the f"
    "irst after the program's own name.\n\n    `Err(e)` when the argument is not UTF-8, `e.offset` being th"
    "e bad byte's offset within it; use\n    `arg_bytes` for such an argument. Faults (`index_out_of_bound"
    "s`) when `i >= arg_count()`.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::env\u{1f}env_var\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn env_var(StringView name) : Result<Optio"
    "n<String>, Utf8Error>\u{1f}The value of the environment variable `name`.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn env_var(StringView na"
    "me) : Result<Option<String>, Utf8Error>\n    in `std::env`\n\n    The value of the environment variable"
    " `name`.\n\n    `Ok(None)` when it is not set; `Err(e)` when its value is not UTF-8, `e` giving the of"
    "fset of the first\n    byte that is not.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::env\u{1f}current_dir\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn current_dir() : Result<S"
    "tring, FileError>\u{1f}The working directory, against which a relative path is resolved.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn curre"
    "nt_dir() : Result<String, FileError>\n    in `std::env`\n\n    The working directory, against which a r"
    "elative path is resolved.\n\n    `Err(Utf8(e))` when its name is not UTF-8.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::env\u{1f}set_current_dir"
    "\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn set_current_dir(StringView path) : Result<void, FileError>\u{1f}Makes `path` the working di"
    "rectory, against which relative paths are resolved from now on.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn set_current_dir(StringVie"
    "w path) : Result<void, FileError>\n    in `std::env`\n\n    Makes `path` the working directory, against"
    " which relative paths are resolved from now on.\n\n    The change is seen by every thread of the progr"
    "am.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::env\u{1f}temp_dir\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn temp_dir() : String\u{1f}The operating system's directory for tempor"
    "ary files.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn temp_dir() : String\n    in `std::env`\n\n    The operating system's directory fo"
    "r temporary files.\n\n    A byte sequence in it that is not UTF-8 is replaced with U+FFFD.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::env\u{1f}"
    "home_dir\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn home_dir() : Option<String>\u{1f}The user's home directory: the variable `HOME`, or"
    " `USERPROFILE` on Windows.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn home_dir() : Option<String>\n    in `std::env`\n\n    The user's "
    "home directory: the variable `HOME`, or `USERPROFILE` on Windows.\n\n    `None` when it is not set. A "
    "byte sequence that is not UTF-8 is replaced with U+FFFD.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::env\u{1f}current_exe\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn current"
    "_exe() : Result<String, FileError>\u{1f}The path of the running program's own executable, absolute and wi"
    "th links followed.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn current_exe() : Result<String, FileError>\n    in `std::env`\n\n    The p"
    "ath of the running program's own executable, absolute and with links followed.\n\n    A program finds "
    "the files installed beside it from here. `Err(Utf8(e))` when the path is not UTF-8;\n    `Err(Io)` wh"
    "en the operating system cannot say.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::env\u{1f}hostname\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn hostname() : Result<String, Fil"
    "eError>\u{1f}The machine's host name, as the operating system knows it.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn hostname() : Result<St"
    "ring, FileError>\n    in `std::env`\n\n    The machine's host name, as the operating system knows it.\n\n"
    "    What a log line or a service's registration names the machine by. `Err(Utf8(e))` when it is not "
    "UTF-8;\n    `Err(Io)` when the operating system cannot say.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::env\u{1f}Args\u{1f}struct\u{1f}export struct Args"
    "\u{1f}The program's arguments, read as options and positional arguments.\u{1f}\u{1f}export struct Args\n    in `std:"
    ":env`\n\n    The program's arguments, read as options and positional arguments.\n\n    Each `Args::flag`"
    ", `Args::option` or `Args::options` call names an option, takes its occurrences from\n    the argumen"
    "ts and adds a line to `Args::help`; `Args::finish` then gives the arguments left and refuses\n    any"
    " option nobody declared. `--name`, `--name=value`, `--name value`, `-s` and `-s value` are read; `--"
    "`\n    ends the options, and a lone `-` is an argument.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::env\u{1f}Args::new\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Args::new(S"
    "tringView usage) : Result<Args, Utf8Error>\u{1f}The program's arguments, from `arg(0)` on, with the usage"
    " line that `Args::help` shows.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Args::new(StringView usage) : Result<Args, Utf8Error>\n    "
    "in `std::env`\n\n    The program's arguments, from `arg(0)` on, with the usage line that `Args::help` "
    "shows.\n\n    The usage line is the text after `Usage: `, such as `prog [options] FILE`.\n\n    `Err(e)`"
    " for the first argument that is not UTF-8.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::env\u{1f}Args::from\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Args::from(Vec<String>"
    " items, StringView usage) : Args\u{1f}The arguments `items`, read as `Args::new` reads the program's own."
    "\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Args::from(Vec<String> items, StringView usage) : Args\n    in `std::env`\n\n    The argume"
    "nts `items`, read as `Args::new` reads the program's own.\n\n    For arguments that come from elsewher"
    "e, or a test.\n\n    Example, \"Args::from: flags, options, the rest\":\n        Vec<String> items = Vec:"
    ":new();\n        Vec::push(&mut items, \"-v\");\n        Vec::push(&mut items, \"--out=a.txt\");\n        V"
    "ec::push(&mut items, \"in.txt\");\n        Args a = Args::from(items, \"prog [options] FILE\");\n        a"
    "ssert(Args::flag(&mut a, \"verbose\", \"v\", \"say more\"));\n        assert(Option::unwrap(Args::option(&m"
    "ut a, \"out\", \"o\", \"FILE\", \"where to write\")) == \"a.txt\");\n        Vec<String> rest = Result::unwrap("
    "Args::finish(&mut a));\n        assert(Vec::len(&rest) == 1 && rest[0] == \"in.txt\");\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::env\u{1f}Args:"
    ":flag\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Args::flag(ref<Args, exclusive> a, str name, str short, str help) : bool\u{1f}Whether "
    "`--name` or `-short` is among the arguments before `--`.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Args::flag(ref<Args, exclusive> "
    "a, str name, str short, str help) : bool\n    in `std::env`\n\n    Whether `--name` or `-short` is amon"
    "g the arguments before `--`.\n\n    `short` is one letter, or `\"\"` for none; `help` is the option's li"
    "ne in `Args::help`. Every occurrence\n    is taken, so `Args::finish` does not see it.\n\n    Example, "
    "\"Args::flag: after --\":\n        Vec<String> items = Vec::new();\n        Vec::push(&mut items, \"--\");"
    "\n        Vec::push(&mut items, \"-v\");\n        Args a = Args::from(items, \"prog [options] FILE\");\n   "
    "     assert(!Args::flag(&mut a, \"verbose\", \"v\", \"say more\"));\n        Vec<String> rest = Result::unw"
    "rap(Args::finish(&mut a));\n        assert(Vec::len(&rest) == 1 && rest[0] == \"-v\");\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::env\u{1f}Args:"
    ":options\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Args::options(ref<Args, exclusive> a, str name, str short, str value_name, str"
    " help) : Vec<String>\u{1f}Every value given for the option `--name` or `-short`, in order.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Arg"
    "s::options(ref<Args, exclusive> a, str name, str short, str value_name, str help) : Vec<String>\n    "
    "in `std::env`\n\n    Every value given for the option `--name` or `-short`, in order.\n\n    A value is "
    "given as `--name=value`, `--name value` or `-s value`; each is taken, the value with it.\n    `value_"
    "name` names the value in `Args::help` (`FILE`). A `--name` or `-s` that is the last argument,\n    ju"
    "st before `--`, or followed by an argument an earlier declaration took (a flag's `-v`), has no value"
    "\n    and is left for `Args::finish` to refuse.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::env\u{1f}Args::option\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Args::option(ref"
    "<Args, exclusive> a, str name, str short, str value_name, str help) : Option<String>\u{1f}The value of th"
    "e option `--name` or `-short`, the last one when it is given more than once.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Args::option"
    "(ref<Args, exclusive> a, str name, str short, str value_name, str help) : Option<String>\n    in `std"
    "::env`\n\n    The value of the option `--name` or `-short`, the last one when it is given more than on"
    "ce.\n\n    `None` when it is not given. Takes its arguments as `Args::options` does.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::env\u{1f}Args::"
    "finish\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Args::finish(ref<Args, exclusive> a) : Result<Vec<String>, String>\u{1f}The arguments"
    " no declaration took, in order, every argument after `--` among them.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Args::finish(ref<Ar"
    "gs, exclusive> a) : Result<Vec<String>, String>\n    in `std::env`\n\n    The arguments no declaration "
    "took, in order, every argument after `--` among them.\n\n    `Err(message)` for the first argument bef"
    "ore `--` that starts with `-` and is not a lone `-`:\n    `unknown option --x`, or `missing a value f"
    "or --x` for a declared option given without its value. The\n    message is for the program to print, "
    "usually followed by `Args::help`.\n\n    Example, \"Args::finish: an undeclared option\":\n        Vec<St"
    "ring> items = Vec::new();\n        Vec::push(&mut items, \"--colour\");\n        Args a = Args::from(ite"
    "ms, \"prog\");\n        match (Args::finish(&mut a))\n        {\n            Err(m) : assert(m == \"unknow"
    "n option --colour\"),\n            Ok(_)  : assert(false),\n        }\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::env\u{1f}Args::help\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export f"
    "n Args::help(ref<Args, shared> a) : String\u{1f}The usage line and one aligned line per option declared s"
    "o far, ready to print.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Args::help(ref<Args, shared> a) : String\n    in `std::env`\n\n    Th"
    "e usage line and one aligned line per option declared so far, ready to print.\n\n    Example, \"Args::h"
    "elp: the layout\":\n        Args a = Args::from(Vec::new(), \"prog FILE\");\n        bool verbose = Args:"
    ":flag(&mut a, \"verbose\", \"v\", \"say more\");\n        Option<String> out = Args::option(&mut a, \"out\", "
    "\"\", \"FILE\", \"where to write\");\n        String h = Args::help(&a);\n        assert(h == \"Usage: prog F"
    "ILE\\n\\nOptions:\\n\"\n                    \"  -v, --verbose   say more\\n\"\n                    \"      --o"
    "ut FILE  where to write\\n\");\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::memory\u{1f}Rc\u{1f}struct\u{1f}export resource struct Rc<T>\u{1f}A reference-counte"
    "d handle to a value on the heap that several owners in one thread share.\u{1f}\u{1f}export resource struct Rc<"
    "T>\n    in `std::memory`\n\n    A reference-counted handle to a value on the heap that several owners i"
    "n one thread share.\n\n    Each `Rc` is one strong handle; the value is destroyed when the last strong"
    " handle is, and the memory is\n    freed once no `Weak` handle remains either. The value is reached o"
    "nly as a shared reference (`Rc::get`),\n    so changing it needs a `mutex` inside. A cycle of `Rc`s i"
    "s never freed: a parent pointer, a cache or an\n    observer list holds a `Weak` instead. `Rc` is not"
    " thread-safe (the count is not synchronised), so `spawn`\n    refuses one, or a value holding or refe"
    "rring to one (`diag.not-thread-safe`), and an `Rc` or `Weak` used in\n    a thread other than the one"
    " that made it (carried there by a closure the compiler could not see through)\n    faults with the sa"
    "me diagnostic; share across threads with `Arc<T>` or `ref<mutex<T>, shared>`.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::memory\u{1f}Rc::new\u{1f}"
    "fn\u{1f}export fn Rc::new<T>(T v) : Rc<T>\u{1f}A new `Rc` holding `v`, the only strong handle to it.\u{1f}\u{1f}export f"
    "n Rc::new<T>(T v) : Rc<T>\n    in `std::memory`\n\n    A new `Rc` holding `v`, the only strong handle t"
    "o it.\n\n    Allocates; faults (`alloc_failure`) when memory cannot be had.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::memory\u{1f}Rc::clone\u{1f}fn"
    "\u{1f}export fn Rc::clone<T>(ref<Rc<T>, shared> r) : Rc<T>\u{1f}A second strong handle to the value `r` holds;"
    " nothing is copied.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Rc::clone<T>(ref<Rc<T>, shared> r) : Rc<T>\n    in `std::memory`\n\n    "
    "A second strong handle to the value `r` holds; nothing is copied.\n\n    What `clone(&r)` calls for an"
    " `Rc`. A reference into the value from `Rc::get` may stay live across it.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::memory\u{1f}Rc::get\u{1f}fn\u{1f}e"
    "xport fn Rc::get<T>(ref<Rc<T>, shared> r) : ref<T, shared>\u{1f}The value `r` holds, as a shared referenc"
    "e.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Rc::get<T>(ref<Rc<T>, shared> r) : ref<T, shared>\n    in `std::memory`\n\n    The value "
    "`r` holds, as a shared reference.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::memory\u{1f}Rc::drop\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Rc::drop<T>(ref<Rc<T>, exclusi"
    "ve> self)\u{1f}Ends one strong handle; run when an `Rc` is destroyed, so programs write `drop(r)` or let "
    "it go.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Rc::drop<T>(ref<Rc<T>, exclusive> self)\n    in `std::memory`\n\n    Ends one strong "
    "handle; run when an `Rc` is destroyed, so programs write `drop(r)` or let it go.\n\n    When this was "
    "the last strong handle the value is destroyed now, and with no `Weak` handle left the\n    memory is "
    "freed too.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::memory\u{1f}Weak\u{1f}struct\u{1f}export resource struct Weak<T>\u{1f}A handle to an `Rc`'s value that"
    " does not keep the value alive.\u{1f}\u{1f}export resource struct Weak<T>\n    in `std::memory`\n\n    A handle t"
    "o an `Rc`'s value that does not keep the value alive.\n\n    It keeps only the memory: once the last `"
    "Rc` ends, the value is gone and `Weak::upgrade` gives `None`.\n    Dropping the last handle of either"
    " kind frees the memory.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::memory\u{1f}Rc::downgrade\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Rc::downgrade<T>(ref<Rc<T>, shared>"
    " r) : Weak<T>\u{1f}A `Weak` handle to the value `r` holds.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Rc::downgrade<T>(ref<Rc<T>, shared>"
    " r) : Weak<T>\n    in `std::memory`\n\n    A `Weak` handle to the value `r` holds.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::memory\u{1f}Weak::"
    "upgrade\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Weak::upgrade<T>(ref<Weak<T>, shared> w) : Option<Rc<T>>\u{1f}A new strong handle to"
    " the value, or `None` when the value has ended.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Weak::upgrade<T>(ref<Weak<T>, shared> w) "
    ": Option<Rc<T>>\n    in `std::memory`\n\n    A new strong handle to the value, or `None` when the value"
    " has ended.\n\n    The value never comes back once ended: after the last `Rc` is destroyed every upgra"
    "de gives `None`.\n\n    Example, \"Weak::upgrade: after the last Rc\":\n        Rc<String> a = Rc::new(\"s"
    "hared\");\n        Rc<String> b = Rc::clone(&a);\n        assert(StringView::len(Rc::get(&b)) == 6);\n  "
    "      Weak<String> w = Rc::downgrade(&a);\n        drop(a);\n        assert(Option::is_some(&Weak::upg"
    "rade(&w)));\n        drop(b);\n        assert(Option::is_none(&Weak::upgrade(&w)));\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::memory\u{1f}Weak"
    "::clone\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Weak::clone<T>(ref<Weak<T>, shared> w) : Weak<T>\u{1f}A second `Weak` handle to the "
    "same value; what `clone(&w)` calls for a `Weak`.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Weak::clone<T>(ref<Weak<T>, shared> w) :"
    " Weak<T>\n    in `std::memory`\n\n    A second `Weak` handle to the same value; what `clone(&w)` calls "
    "for a `Weak`.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::memory\u{1f}Weak::drop\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Weak::drop<T>(ref<Weak<T>, exclusive> self)\u{1f}Ends"
    " one weak handle; run when a `Weak` is destroyed.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Weak::drop<T>(ref<Weak<T>, exclusive> s"
    "elf)\n    in `std::memory`\n\n    Ends one weak handle; run when a `Weak` is destroyed.\n\n    When no st"
    "rong or weak handle remains, the memory is freed.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::memory\u{1f}Arc\u{1f}struct\u{1f}export resource struct Ar"
    "c<T>\u{1f}A reference-counted handle to a value that several threads share.\u{1f}\u{1f}export resource struct Arc<T"
    ">\n    in `std::memory`\n\n    A reference-counted handle to a value that several threads share.\n\n    `"
    "Rc` for threads: each `Arc` is one strong handle, and handles may be cloned into other threads, move"
    "d and\n    dropped there, so a thread owns its share of the value and may outlive the frame that made"
    " it, which a\n    reference to the value does not allow. The counts are atomic, so clones and drops f"
    "rom several threads at\n    once lose nothing; the value is destroyed, once, by whichever handle ends"
    " last. It is reached only as a\n    shared reference (`Arc::get`), so changing it needs a `mutex` or "
    "an `Atomic` inside. An `ArcWeak`\n    (`Arc::downgrade`) keeps the box but not the value, for a paren"
    "t pointer or a cache.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::memory\u{1f}Arc::new\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Arc::new<T>(T v) : Arc<T>\u{1f}A new `Arc` hold"
    "ing `v`, the only strong handle to it.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Arc::new<T>(T v) : Arc<T>\n    in `std::memory`\n\n  "
    "  A new `Arc` holding `v`, the only strong handle to it.\n\n    Allocates; faults (`alloc_failure`) wh"
    "en memory cannot be had.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::memory\u{1f}Arc::clone\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Arc::clone<T>(ref<Arc<T>, shared> a) "
    ": Arc<T>\u{1f}A second strong handle to the value `a` holds; nothing is copied.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Arc::clone<T>("
    "ref<Arc<T>, shared> a) : Arc<T>\n    in `std::memory`\n\n    A second strong handle to the value `a` ho"
    "lds; nothing is copied.\n\n    What `clone(&a)` calls for an `Arc`. The new handle may go to another t"
    "hread.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::memory\u{1f}Arc::get\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Arc::get<T>(ref<Arc<T>, shared> a) : ref<T, shared>\u{1f}The v"
    "alue `a` holds, as a shared reference.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Arc::get<T>(ref<Arc<T>, shared> a) : ref<T, shared"
    ">\n    in `std::memory`\n\n    The value `a` holds, as a shared reference.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::memory\u{1f}Arc::drop\u{1f}fn\u{1f}e"
    "xport fn Arc::drop<T>(ref<Arc<T>, exclusive> self)\u{1f}Ends one strong handle; run when an `Arc` is dest"
    "royed, so programs write `drop(a)` or let it go.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Arc::drop<T>(ref<Arc<T>, exclusive> self"
    ")\n    in `std::memory`\n\n    Ends one strong handle; run when an `Arc` is destroyed, so programs writ"
    "e `drop(a)` or let it go.\n\n    When this was the last strong handle, on whichever thread, the value "
    "is destroyed now, and with no\n    `ArcWeak` handle left the memory is freed too.\n\n    Example, \"Arc:"
    " shared by threads, destroyed once\":\n        Arc<mutex<u32>> a = Arc::new(Mutex::new(0: u32));\n     "
    "   Vec<handle<void>> hs = Vec::new();\n        for (u32 i = 0; i < 4; i += 1)\n        {\n            V"
    "ec::push(&mut hs, spawn(move [](Arc<mutex<u32>> h)\n            {\n                auto g = lock(Arc::"
    "get(&h));\n                *g += 1;\n            }, Arc::clone(&a)));\n        }\n        ArcWeak<mutex<"
    "u32>> w = Arc::downgrade(&a);\n        drop(a);                                  // the workers' hand"
    "les keep the value\n        foreach (h in hs)\n        {\n            join(h);\n        }\n        assert"
    "(Option::is_none(&ArcWeak::upgrade(&w)));   // the last worker's drop ended it\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::memory\u{1f}ArcWeak"
    "\u{1f}struct\u{1f}export resource struct ArcWeak<T>\u{1f}A handle that does not keep the value alive.\u{1f}\u{1f}export resou"
    "rce struct ArcWeak<T>\n    in `std::memory`\n\n    A handle that does not keep the value alive.\n\n    As"
    " `Weak` is to `Rc`: it keeps only the memory. Once the last `Arc` ends, the value is gone and\n    `A"
    "rcWeak::upgrade` gives `None`. Dropping the last handle of either kind frees the memory.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::memo"
    "ry\u{1f}Arc::downgrade\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Arc::downgrade<T>(ref<Arc<T>, shared> a) : ArcWeak<T>\u{1f}An `ArcWeak` ha"
    "ndle to the value `a` holds.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Arc::downgrade<T>(ref<Arc<T>, shared> a) : ArcWeak<T>\n    in"
    " `std::memory`\n\n    An `ArcWeak` handle to the value `a` holds.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::memory\u{1f}ArcWeak::upgrade\u{1f}fn\u{1f}ex"
    "port fn ArcWeak::upgrade<T>(ref<ArcWeak<T>, shared> w) : Option<Arc<T>>\u{1f}A new strong handle to the v"
    "alue, or `None` when the value has ended.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn ArcWeak::upgrade<T>(ref<ArcWeak<T>, shared> w) "
    ": Option<Arc<T>>\n    in `std::memory`\n\n    A new strong handle to the value, or `None` when the valu"
    "e has ended.\n\n    The value never comes back once ended: after the last `Arc` is destroyed every upg"
    "rade gives `None`.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::memory\u{1f}ArcWeak::clone\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn ArcWeak::clone<T>(ref<ArcWeak<T>, share"
    "d> w) : ArcWeak<T>\u{1f}A second `ArcWeak` handle to the same value; what `clone(&w)` calls for an `ArcWe"
    "ak`.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn ArcWeak::clone<T>(ref<ArcWeak<T>, shared> w) : ArcWeak<T>\n    in `std::memory`\n\n    "
    "A second `ArcWeak` handle to the same value; what `clone(&w)` calls for an `ArcWeak`.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::memory\u{1f}"
    "ArcWeak::drop\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn ArcWeak::drop<T>(ref<ArcWeak<T>, exclusive> self)\u{1f}Ends one weak handle; r"
    "un when an `ArcWeak` is destroyed.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn ArcWeak::drop<T>(ref<ArcWeak<T>, exclusive> self)\n    "
    "in `std::memory`\n\n    Ends one weak handle; run when an `ArcWeak` is destroyed.\n\n    When no strong "
    "or weak handle remains, the memory is freed.\n\n    Example, \"ArcWeak::upgrade: after the last Arc\":\n "
    "       Arc<String> a = Arc::new(\"shared\");\n        Arc<String> b = clone(&a);\n        assert(StringV"
    "iew::len(Arc::get(&b)) == 6);\n        ArcWeak<String> w = Arc::downgrade(&a);\n        ArcWeak<String"
    "> w2 = clone(&w);\n        drop(a);\n        assert(Option::is_some(&ArcWeak::upgrade(&w)));\n        d"
    "rop(b);\n        assert(Option::is_none(&ArcWeak::upgrade(&w)) && Option::is_none(&ArcWeak::upgrade(&"
    "w2)));\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::memory\u{1f}Box\u{1f}struct\u{1f}export resource struct Box<T>\u{1f}A value on the heap with one owner.\u{1f}\u{1f}e"
    "xport resource struct Box<T>\n    in `std::memory`\n\n    A value on the heap with one owner.\n\n    It g"
    "ives recursive types their indirection (`struct Node { i32 v; Option<Box<Node>> next; }`) and moves "
    "a\n    large value as a pointer. A `Box` is a resource: it moves, and destroying it destroys the valu"
    "e and frees\n    the memory. It allocates `sizeof<T>()` bytes, or one for a zero-sized `T`. A `Box` i"
    "s one pointer (D-0298):\n    `Box::new` never gives the null pointer, so null marks a `Box` whose val"
    "ue `into_inner` took.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::memory\u{1f}Box::new\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Box::new<T>(T v) : Box<T>\u{1f}A new `Box` hold"
    "ing `v`.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Box::new<T>(T v) : Box<T>\n    in `std::memory`\n\n    A new `Box` holding `v`.\n\n  "
    "  Allocates; faults (`alloc_failure`) when memory cannot be had.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::memory\u{1f}Box::get\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn"
    " Box::get<T>(ref<Box<T>, shared> b) : ref<T, shared>\u{1f}The value in `b`, as a shared reference.\u{1f}\u{1f}expor"
    "t fn Box::get<T>(ref<Box<T>, shared> b) : ref<T, shared>\n    in `std::memory`\n\n    The value in `b`,"
    " as a shared reference.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::memory\u{1f}Box::get_mut\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Box::get_mut<T>(ref<Box<T>, exclusiv"
    "e> b) : ref<T, exclusive>\u{1f}The value in `b`, as an exclusive reference.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Box::get_mut<T>(re"
    "f<Box<T>, exclusive> b) : ref<T, exclusive>\n    in `std::memory`\n\n    The value in `b`, as an exclus"
    "ive reference.\n\n    Needs an exclusive borrow of the `Box`, as changing any value does.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::memor"
    "y\u{1f}Box::clone\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Box::clone<T: clone>(ref<Box<T>, shared> b) : Box<T>\u{1f}A new `Box` holding a"
    " copy of the value in `b`; what `clone(&b)` calls for a `Box`.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Box::clone<T: clone>(ref<B"
    "ox<T>, shared> b) : Box<T>\n    in `std::memory`\n\n    A new `Box` holding a copy of the value in `b`;"
    " what `clone(&b)` calls for a `Box`.\n\n    A chain of boxes, whose type links to one more of itself ("
    "`Branch(Box<Tree>)`, a list of\n    `Option<Box<Node>>`), is copied by its derived `clone` at any len"
    "gth: past a thousand links\n    it copies the rest with a loop. So is a tree whose values have two or"
    " more such links (a struct's\n    `left` and `right`, a variant's `Add { Box<Expr> a; Box<Expr> b; }`"
    "): past a thousand levels it\n    copies the rest with a loop over a stack of its own. A type that li"
    "nks to itself only through\n    another type (`Node(Box<Pair>)`), or one with a `clone` of its own, t"
    "akes a call for each level,\n    so millions of levels can exhaust the stack (`diag.stack-exhausted`)"
    ".\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::memory\u{1f}Box::into_inner\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Box::into_inner<T>(Box<T> b) : T\u{1f}The value in `b`, move"
    "d out; the `Box`'s memory is freed.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Box::into_inner<T>(Box<T> b) : T\n    in `std::memory`"
    "\n\n    The value in `b`, moved out; the `Box`'s memory is freed.\n\n    Faults if another thread holds "
    "an exclusive path to the value.\n\n    Example, \"Box::into_inner: get_mut, clone and into_inner\":\n    "
    "    Box<i32> b = Box::new(5);\n        *Box::get_mut(&mut b) = 6;\n        assert(*Box::get(&b) == 6);"
    "\n        Box<i32> c = clone(&b);\n        assert(Box::into_inner(c) == 6);\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::memory\u{1f}Box::drop\u{1f}fn"
    "\u{1f}export fn Box::drop<T>(ref<Box<T>, exclusive> self)\u{1f}Destroys the value and frees the memory; run wh"
    "en a `Box` is destroyed.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Box::drop<T>(ref<Box<T>, exclusive> self)\n    in `std::memory`\n\n"
    "    Destroys the value and frees the memory; run when a `Box` is destroyed.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::sync\u{1f}Channel\u{1f}stru"
    "ct\u{1f}export resource struct Channel<T>\u{1f}A bounded first-in, first-out queue of values between threads.\u{1f}"
    "\u{1f}export resource struct Channel<T>\n    in `std::sync`\n\n    A bounded first-in, first-out queue of va"
    "lues between threads.\n\n    Shared by reference, as a `mutex` is: every thread works on it through `r"
    "ef<Channel<T>, shared>`. `send`\n    waits while it is full and `recv` while it is empty; after `clos"
    "e`, `send` gives its value back and `recv`\n    drains what is left, then gives `None`. Destroying th"
    "e channel destroys the values still in it, oldest\n    first.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::sync\u{1f}Channel::new\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn C"
    "hannel::new<T>(usize cap) : Channel<T>\u{1f}An empty open channel holding at most `cap` values.\u{1f}\u{1f}export f"
    "n Channel::new<T>(usize cap) : Channel<T>\n    in `std::sync`\n    requires\n        cap != 0, channel_"
    "zero_capacity\n\n    An empty open channel holding at most `cap` values.\n\n    Faults (`channel_zero_ca"
    "pacity`) when `cap` is 0; 1 makes a hand-over.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::sync\u{1f}Channel::send\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Channel::send<"
    "T>(ref<Channel<T>, shared> ch, T v) : Result<void, T>\u{1f}Puts `v` at the back of `ch`, waiting while th"
    "e channel is full.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Channel::send<T>(ref<Channel<T>, shared> ch, T v) : Result<void, T>\n  "
    "  in `std::sync`\n\n    Puts `v` at the back of `ch`, waiting while the channel is full.\n\n    `Err(v)`"
    ", the value handed back, once the channel is closed. Exclusive references inside `v` are lent to\n   "
    " the receiving thread: the sender's own copies of them clash with them while they live.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::sync\u{1f}"
    "Channel::recv\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Channel::recv<T>(ref<Channel<T>, shared> ch) : Option<T>\u{1f}Takes the value "
    "at the front of `ch`, waiting while the channel is empty.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Channel::recv<T>(ref<Channel<T>"
    ", shared> ch) : Option<T>\n    in `std::sync`\n\n    Takes the value at the front of `ch`, waiting whil"
    "e the channel is empty.\n\n    `None` once the channel is closed and empty.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::sync\u{1f}Channel::try_r"
    "ecv\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Channel::try_recv<T>(ref<Channel<T>, shared> ch) : Result<Option<T>, void>\u{1f}Takes th"
    "e value at the front of `ch` if one is waiting, without waiting.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Channel::try_recv<T>(ref"
    "<Channel<T>, shared> ch) : Result<Option<T>, void>\n    in `std::sync`\n\n    Takes the value at the fr"
    "ont of `ch` if one is waiting, without waiting.\n\n    `Ok(Some(v))` with the value; `Ok(None)` when t"
    "he channel is empty; `Err(())` once it is closed and empty,\n    so a loop that also polls something "
    "else (a socket, `interrupt_requested`) knows when the senders are\n    done.\n\n    Example, \"Channel::"
    "try_recv: the three outcomes\":\n        Channel<i32> ch = Channel::new(1);\n        assert(Option::is_"
    "none(&Result::unwrap(Channel::try_recv(&ch))));\n        _ = Channel::send(&ch, 5);\n        Channel::"
    "close(&ch);\n        assert(Option::unwrap(Result::unwrap(Channel::try_recv(&ch))) == 5);\n        ass"
    "ert(Result::is_err(&Channel::try_recv(&ch)));\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::sync\u{1f}Channel::recv_timeout_ms\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Chan"
    "nel::recv_timeout_ms<T>(ref<Channel<T>, shared> ch, u64 ms) : Result<Option<T>, void>\u{1f}Takes the valu"
    "e at the front of `ch`, waiting at most `ms` milliseconds for one.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Channel::recv_timeout_"
    "ms<T>(ref<Channel<T>, shared> ch, u64 ms) : Result<Option<T>, void>\n    in `std::sync`\n\n    Takes th"
    "e value at the front of `ch`, waiting at most `ms` milliseconds for one.\n\n    `Ok(Some(v))` with the"
    " value; `Ok(None)` when none arrived in time; `Err(())` when the channel is closed\n    and empty. Th"
    "e wait is a poll (every millisecond at first, then every five), so it ends at most a few\n    millise"
    "conds late.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::sync\u{1f}cpu_count\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn cpu_count() : usize\u{1f}How many threads the machine runs"
    " at once, at least 1: the size of a worker pool.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn cpu_count() : usize\n    in `std::sync`\n\n"
    "    How many threads the machine runs at once, at least 1: the size of a worker pool.\n\n    The proce"
    "ssors, or what this program may use of them.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::sync\u{1f}Channel::close\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Channel::close<"
    "T>(ref<Channel<T>, shared> ch)\u{1f}Marks `ch` as taking no more values.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Channel::close<T>(ref"
    "<Channel<T>, shared> ch)\n    in `std::sync`\n\n    Marks `ch` as taking no more values.\n\n    `send` fa"
    "ils from then on, and `recv` gives `None` once the values already in it are taken. Waiting\n    sende"
    "rs and receivers wake. Closing a closed channel does nothing.\n\n    Example, \"Channel::close: drainin"
    "g a closed channel\":\n        Channel<i32> ch = Channel::new(2);\n        assert(Result::is_ok(&Channe"
    "l::send(&ch, 1)));\n        assert(Result::is_ok(&Channel::send(&ch, 2)));\n        Channel::close(&ch"
    ");\n        assert(Result::is_err(&Channel::send(&ch, 3)));\n        assert(Option::unwrap(Channel::re"
    "cv(&ch)) == 1);\n        assert(Option::unwrap(Channel::recv(&ch)) == 2);\n        assert(Option::is_n"
    "one(&Channel::recv(&ch)));\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::sync\u{1f}Channel::drop\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Channel::drop<T>(ref<Channel<T>, e"
    "xclusive> self)\u{1f}Destroys the values still in the channel, oldest first; run when a `Channel` is dest"
    "royed.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Channel::drop<T>(ref<Channel<T>, exclusive> self)\n    in `std::sync`\n\n    Destroys"
    " the values still in the channel, oldest first; run when a `Channel` is destroyed.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::sync\u{1f}Threa"
    "dFailure\u{1f}struct\u{1f}export struct ThreadFailure\u{1f}A spawned thread's fault, contained and handed over by `"
    "try_join` instead of raised again.\u{1f}\u{1f}export struct ThreadFailure\n    in `std::sync`\n\n    A spawned th"
    "read's fault, contained and handed over by `try_join` instead of raised again.\n\n    Its text is kept"
    " by the runtime until the program ends.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::sync\u{1f}try_join\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn try_join<R>(handle<R> h) :"
    " Result<R, ThreadFailure>\u{1f}Waits for the thread `h` and gives its result, or its failure as a value i"
    "nstead of raising it again.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn try_join<R>(handle<R> h) : Result<R, ThreadFailure>\n    in `s"
    "td::sync`\n\n    Waits for the thread `h` and gives its result, or its failure as a value instead of r"
    "aising it again.\n\n    `Ok` of the thread's result; `Err(ThreadFailure)` when the thread faulted or w"
    "as cancelled, and the\n    failure is then taken, so nothing is raised. How a supervisor keeps going "
    "when a worker has a bug; `join`\n    raises the failure instead.\n\n    Example, \"try_join: a contained"
    " fault\":\n        Option<i32> none = None;\n        auto h = spawn(Option::unwrap<i32>, none);\n       "
    " match (try_join(h))\n        {\n            Ok(_)  : assert(false),\n            Err(f) :\n            "
    "{\n                String d = ThreadFailure::diagnostic(&f);\n                assert(d == \"diag.unwrap"
    "-failed\");\n                assert(!ThreadFailure::is_cancelled(&f));\n            },\n        }\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std:"
    ":sync\u{1f}is_finished\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn is_finished<R>(ref<handle<R>, shared> h) : bool\u{1f}Whether the thread `h"
    "` has ended, its result or failure waiting for `join` or `try_join`.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn is_finished<R>(ref<h"
    "andle<R>, shared> h) : bool\n    in `std::sync`\n\n    Whether the thread `h` has ended, its result or "
    "failure waiting for `join` or `try_join`.\n\n    Never waits and takes nothing: how a supervisor finds"
    " which of its threads to take.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::sync\u{1f}ThreadFailure::text\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn ThreadFailure::text(ref<"
    "ThreadFailure, shared> f) : String\u{1f}The failure's report, as the program would have printed it had no"
    "thing taken the failure.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn ThreadFailure::text(ref<ThreadFailure, shared> f) : String\n    i"
    "n `std::sync`\n\n    The failure's report, as the program would have printed it had nothing taken the "
    "failure.\n\n    As a fault's report on standard error: its first line names the diagnostic, its second"
    " the location.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::sync\u{1f}ThreadFailure::diagnostic\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn ThreadFailure::diagnostic(ref<Thre"
    "adFailure, shared> f) : String\u{1f}The name of the failure's diagnostic alone, such as `diag.index-out-o"
    "f-bounds` or `diag.thread-cancelled`.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn ThreadFailure::diagnostic(ref<ThreadFailure, shared"
    "> f) : String\n    in `std::sync`\n\n    The name of the failure's diagnostic alone, such as `diag.inde"
    "x-out-of-bounds` or `diag.thread-cancelled`.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::sync\u{1f}ThreadFailure::is_cancelled\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Th"
    "readFailure::is_cancelled(ref<ThreadFailure, shared> f) : bool\u{1f}Whether the thread ended because it w"
    "as cancelled, rather than by a fault of its own.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn ThreadFailure::is_cancelled(ref<ThreadFa"
    "ilure, shared> f) : bool\n    in `std::sync`\n\n    Whether the thread ended because it was cancelled, "
    "rather than by a fault of its own.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::sync\u{1f}Atomic\u{1f}struct\u{1f}export resource struct Atomic<T>\u{1f}An int"
    "eger that threads share and change without a lock.\u{1f}\u{1f}export resource struct Atomic<T>\n    in `std::sy"
    "nc`\n\n    An integer that threads share and change without a lock.\n\n    Shared by reference, as a `mu"
    "tex` is: every operation takes `ref<Atomic<T>, shared>`, and each one is\n    indivisible and sequent"
    "ially consistent, so all threads see all operations on all atomics in one order.\n    `T` is an integ"
    "er type of at most 64 bits. For a counter, a flag (an `Atomic<u8>`), a sequence number or a\n    maxi"
    "mum kept by several threads; state of more than one integer belongs in a `mutex`.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::sync\u{1f}Atomic"
    "::new\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Atomic::new<T: integer>(T v) : Atomic<T>\u{1f}A new atomic holding `v`.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Ato"
