    " ref<BigUint, shared> m) : BigUint\n    in `std::math`\n\n    `base` raised to the power `exp`, modulo "
    "`m`.\n\n    By squaring and multiplying, reducing at each step. Faults (`div_by_zero`) when `m` is 0; "
    "anything modulo\n    1 is 0.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::math\u{1f}BigUint::clone\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn BigUint::clone(ref<BigUint, shar"
    "ed> a) : BigUint\u{1f}A copy of `a`; what `clone(&a)` calls for a `BigUint`.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn BigUint::clone(re"
    "f<BigUint, shared> a) : BigUint\n    in `std::math`\n\n    A copy of `a`; what `clone(&a)` calls for a "
    "`BigUint`.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::math\u{1f}BigUint::mod_inverse\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn BigUint::mod_inverse(ref<BigUint, shared> a"
    ", ref<BigUint, shared> m) : Option<BigUint>\u{1f}The inverse of `a` modulo `m`: the number below `m` whos"
    "e product with `a` is 1 modulo `m`.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn BigUint::mod_inverse(ref<BigUint, shared> a, ref<BigU"
    "int, shared> m) : Option<BigUint>\n    in `std::math`\n\n    The inverse of `a` modulo `m`: the number "
    "below `m` whose product with `a` is 1 modulo `m`.\n\n    `None` when `a` and `m` share a factor above "
    "1, or when `m` is below 2. Its time depends on the values,\n    as every `BigUint` operation's does: "
    "for public numbers and one-time blinding factors, not a private key.\n\n    Example, \"BigUint::mod_inv"
    "erse: with and without an inverse\":\n        BigUint m = BigUint::from_u64(11);\n        match (BigUin"
    "t::mod_inverse(&BigUint::from_u64(3), &m))\n        {\n            Some(x) : assert(BigUint::eq(&x, &B"
    "igUint::from_u64(4))),\n            None    : assert(false),\n        }\n        assert(Option::is_none"
    "(&BigUint::mod_inverse(&BigUint::from_u64(6), &BigUint::from_u64(9))));\n        BigUint p = BigUint:"
    ":mod_pow(&BigUint::from_u64(3), &BigUint::from_u64(10), &m);\n        assert(BigUint::eq(&p, &BigUint"
    "::from_u64(1)));\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::math\u{1f}BigUint::text\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn BigUint::text(ref<BigUint, shared> a) : Stri"
    "ng\u{1f}`a` in decimal digits, with no leading zero.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn BigUint::text(ref<BigUint, shared> a) : S"
    "tring\n    in `std::math`\n\n    `a` in decimal digits, with no leading zero.\n\n    Example, \"BigUint::t"
    "ext: past u64 and back\":\n        BigUint big = BigUint::shift_left(&BigUint::from_u64(1), 100);\n    "
    "    assert(BigUint::bit_len(&big) == 101);\n        String t = BigUint::text(&big);\n        assert(t "
    "== \"1267650600228229401496703205376\");\n        BigUint back = Result::unwrap(BigUint::parse(&t));\n  "
    "      assert(BigUint::eq(&back, &big));\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::math\u{1f}BigUint::parse\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn BigUint::parse(Strin"
    "gView text) : Result<BigUint, ParseError>\u{1f}The number written in decimal digits in `text`.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn"
    " BigUint::parse(StringView text) : Result<BigUint, ParseError>\n    in `std::math`\n\n    The number wr"
    "itten in decimal digits in `text`.\n\n    `Err(Empty)` when `text` is empty; `Err(Invalid(i))` at the "
    "first byte `i` that is not a digit (a sign is\n    not accepted).\n\n    Example, \"BigUint::parse: the "
    "position of a bad byte\":\n        match (BigUint::parse(\"12x\"))\n        {\n            Ok(_) : assert("
    "false),\n            Err(e) : match (e)\n            {\n                Invalid(i) : assert(i == 2),\n  "
    "              _          : assert(false),\n            },\n        }\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::stats\u{1f}sum\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn sum"
    "<T: number>(slice<T, shared> data) : T\u{1f}The sum of `data`'s values in their own type; 0 for an empty "
    "slice.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn sum<T: number>(slice<T, shared> data) : T\n    in `std::stats`\n\n    The sum of `dat"
    "a`'s values in their own type; 0 for an empty slice.\n\n    Added left to right with `+`, so an intege"
    "r sum that does not fit in `T` faults (`arith_overflow`),\n    as `+` does. For the sum as an `f64` w"
    "hatever `T` is, multiply `mean` by the count.\n\n    Example, \"sum: in the slice's own type\":\n        "
    "array<i32, 4> a = [3, -1, 4, 1];\n        assert(sum(&a) == 7);\n        assert(sum(&a[0..0]) == 0);\n "
    "       array<f64, 3> f = [0.5, 0.25, 2.0];\n        assert(sum(&f) == 2.75);\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::stats\u{1f}mean\u{1f}fn\u{1f}exp"
    "ort fn mean<T: number>(slice<T, shared> data) : Option<f64>\u{1f}The arithmetic mean of `data`'s values; "
    "`None` for an empty slice.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn mean<T: number>(slice<T, shared> data) : Option<f64>\n    in `s"
    "td::stats`\n\n    The arithmetic mean of `data`'s values; `None` for an empty slice.\n\n    Each value i"
    "s converted to `f64` with `to_float` and the values added left to right, then divided by\n    the cou"
    "nt. A NaN among the values makes the mean a NaN.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::stats\u{1f}variance\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn variance<T: numb"
    "er>(slice<T, shared> data) : Option<f64>\u{1f}The population variance of `data`'s values, the mean of the"
    " squared deviations from their mean; `None` for an empty slice.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn variance<T: number>(slice"
    "<T, shared> data) : Option<f64>\n    in `std::stats`\n\n    The population variance of `data`'s values,"
    " the mean of the squared deviations from their mean;\n    `None` for an empty slice.\n\n    Two passes:"
    " the mean first, then the sum of `(x - mean)²` left to right, divided by the count. For\n    the var"
    "iance of a sample drawn from a larger population, use `sample_variance`.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::stats\u{1f}stddev\u{1f}fn\u{1f}expo"
    "rt fn stddev<T: number>(slice<T, shared> data) : Option<f64>\u{1f}The population standard deviation of `d"
    "ata`'s values, the square root of `variance`; `None` for an empty slice.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn stddev<T: number"
    ">(slice<T, shared> data) : Option<f64>\n    in `std::stats`\n\n    The population standard deviation of"
    " `data`'s values, the square root of `variance`; `None` for an\n    empty slice.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::stats\u{1f}sample_"
    "variance\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn sample_variance<T: number>(slice<T, shared> data) : Option<f64>\u{1f}The sample var"
    "iance of `data`'s values, the squared deviations from their mean divided by one less than the count "
    "(Bessel's correction); `None` for fewer than two values.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn sample_variance<T: number>(slice"
    "<T, shared> data) : Option<f64>\n    in `std::stats`\n\n    The sample variance of `data`'s values, the"
    " squared deviations from their mean divided by one less\n    than the count (Bessel's correction); `N"
    "one` for fewer than two values.\n\n    For values that are the whole population, use `variance`.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std"
    "::stats\u{1f}sample_stddev\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn sample_stddev<T: number>(slice<T, shared> data) : Option<f64>\u{1f}The"
    " sample standard deviation of `data`'s values, the square root of `sample_variance`; `None` for fewe"
    "r than two values.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn sample_stddev<T: number>(slice<T, shared> data) : Option<f64>\n    in `"
    "std::stats`\n\n    The sample standard deviation of `data`'s values, the square root of `sample_varian"
    "ce`; `None` for\n    fewer than two values.\n\n    Example, \"variance: population and sample\":\n        "
    "array<u32, 8> a = [2, 4, 4, 4, 5, 5, 7, 9];\n        assert(Option::unwrap(variance(&a)) == 4.0);\n   "
    "     assert(Option::unwrap(stddev(&a)) == 2.0);\n        assert(Option::unwrap(sample_variance(&a)) ="
    "= 32.0 / 7.0);\n        assert(Option::is_none(&variance(&a[0..0])));\n        assert(Option::unwrap(v"
    "ariance(&a[0..1])) == 0.0);\n        assert(Option::is_none(&sample_variance(&a[0..1])));\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::stat"
    "s\u{1f}median\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn median<T: number>(slice<T, shared> data) : Option<f64>\u{1f}The median of `data`'s "
    "values: the middle one of them sorted, or the mean of the two middle ones when the count is even; `N"
    "one` for an empty slice.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn median<T: number>(slice<T, shared> data) : Option<f64>\n    in `s"
    "td::stats`\n\n    The median of `data`'s values: the middle one of them sorted, or the mean of the two"
    " middle ones\n    when the count is even; `None` for an empty slice.\n\n    The values are copied, conv"
    "erted to `f64` and sorted as `Vec::sort` sorts floats (every NaN after\n    every number); `data` is "
    "not changed.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::stats\u{1f}percentile\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn percentile<T: number>(slice<T, shared> data, f64 p"
    ") : Option<f64>\u{1f}The `p`th percentile of `data`'s values, `p` from 0 to 100, with linear interpolatio"
    "n between the two sorted values it falls between; `None` for an empty slice.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn percentile<T"
    ": number>(slice<T, shared> data, f64 p) : Option<f64>\n    in `std::stats`\n    requires\n        p >= "
    "0.0 && p <= 100.0, assert_failed, \"percentile: `p` is outside 0 to 100\"\n\n    The `p`th percentile of"
    " `data`'s values, `p` from 0 to 100, with linear interpolation between the\n    two sorted values it "
    "falls between; `None` for an empty slice.\n\n    The position is `(count - 1) · p / 100` in the sorte"
    "d values (R's type 7, the default of spreadsheets\n    and NumPy): `percentile(data, 0.0)` is the min"
    "imum, `percentile(data, 100.0)` the maximum, and\n    `percentile(data, 50.0)` the median. Faults (`a"
    "ssert_failed`) when `p` is below 0, above 100 or a NaN.\n\n    Example, \"percentile: interpolated betw"
    "een neighbours\":\n        array<i64, 5> a = [15, 20, 35, 40, 50];\n        assert(Option::unwrap(perce"
    "ntile(&a, 25.0)) == 20.0);\n        assert(Option::unwrap(percentile(&a, 40.0)) == 29.0);\n        ass"
    "ert(Option::unwrap(percentile(&a, 50.0)) == 35.0);\n        assert(Option::unwrap(median(&a)) == 35.0"
    ");\n        assert(Option::unwrap(median(&a[0..4])) == 27.5);\n        assert(Option::unwrap(percentil"
    "e(&a, 0.0)) == 15.0);\n        assert(Option::unwrap(percentile(&a, 100.0)) == 50.0);\n        assert("
    "Option::is_none(&median(&a[0..0])));\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::stats\u{1f}minimum\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn minimum<T: number>(slice<T, s"
    "hared> data) : Option<f64>\u{1f}The smallest of `data`'s values as an `f64`; `None` for an empty slice.\u{1f}\u{1f}"
    "export fn minimum<T: number>(slice<T, shared> data) : Option<f64>\n    in `std::stats`\n\n    The small"
    "est of `data`'s values as an `f64`; `None` for an empty slice.\n\n    A NaN is passed over: the result"
    " is a NaN only when every value is one.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::stats\u{1f}maximum\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn maximum<T: number>(slice<T"
    ", shared> data) : Option<f64>\u{1f}The largest of `data`'s values as an `f64`; `None` for an empty slice."
    "\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn maximum<T: number>(slice<T, shared> data) : Option<f64>\n    in `std::stats`\n\n    The lar"
    "gest of `data`'s values as an `f64`; `None` for an empty slice.\n\n    A NaN is passed over: the resul"
    "t is a NaN only when every value is one.\n\n    Example, \"minimum and maximum: as f64, a NaN passed ov"
    "er\":\n        array<i16, 4> a = [7, -3, 12, 0];\n        assert(Option::unwrap(minimum(&a)) == -3.0);\n"
    "        assert(Option::unwrap(maximum(&a)) == 12.0);\n        f64 nan = 0.0 / 0.0;\n        array<f64,"
    " 3> f = [nan, 2.5, 1.5];\n        assert(Option::unwrap(minimum(&f)) == 1.5);\n        assert(Option::"
    "unwrap(maximum(&f)) == 2.5);\n        assert(Option::is_none(&minimum(&f[0..0])));\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::stats\u{1f}Summa"
    "ry\u{1f}struct\u{1f}export struct Summary\u{1f}What `describe` gives: the count of the values and their minimum, ma"
    "ximum, mean, population standard deviation and median.\u{1f}\u{1f}export struct Summary\n    in `std::stats`\n\n "
    "   What `describe` gives: the count of the values and their minimum, maximum, mean, population stand"
    "ard\n    deviation and median.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::stats\u{1f}describe\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn describe<T: number>(slice<T, shared"
    "> data) : Option<Summary>\u{1f}The count, `minimum`, `maximum`, `mean`, `stddev` and `median` of `data`'s"
    " values as one `Summary`; `None` for an empty slice.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn describe<T: number>(slice<T, shared>"
    " data) : Option<Summary>\n    in `std::stats`\n\n    The count, `minimum`, `maximum`, `mean`, `stddev` "
    "and `median` of `data`'s values as one `Summary`;\n    `None` for an empty slice.\n\n    Example, \"desc"
    "ribe: a run of timings\":\n        array<u32, 6> ms = [12, 15, 11, 40, 13, 14];\n        Summary s = Op"
    "tion::unwrap(describe(&ms));\n        assert(s.count == 6 && s.minimum == 11.0 && s.maximum == 40.0);"
    "\n        assert(s.mean == 17.5 && s.median == 13.5);\n        assert(s.stddev == sqrt(Option::unwrap("
    "variance(&ms))));\n        assert(Option::is_none(&describe(&ms[0..0])));\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::time\u{1f}DateTime\u{1f}struct"
    "\u{1f}export struct DateTime\u{1f}A moment in UTC: a date of the proleptic Gregorian calendar and a time of da"
    "y to the second.\u{1f}\u{1f}export struct DateTime\n    in `std::time`\n\n    A moment in UTC: a date of the prol"
    "eptic Gregorian calendar and a time of day to the second.\n\n    `year` 0 is 1 BC and -1 is 2 BC; ther"
    "e are no leap seconds, so every day has 86,400 seconds. Any field\n    values can be written in a str"
    "uct literal: `DateTime::new` checks them, and `DateTime::to_unix` faults\n    on a value that names n"
    "o moment. Keep moments in UTC and convert with `DateTime::to_local` only for\n    display.\n\n    Examp"
    "le, \"DateTime: a negative year\":\n        DateTime t = DateTime { .year = -1, .month = 3, .day = 1, ."
    "hour = 9, .minute = 5, .second = 0 };\n        String s = DateTime::to_iso(&t);\n        assert(s == \""
    "-0001-03-01T09:05:00Z\");\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::time\u{1f}Weekday\u{1f}enum\u{1f}export enum Weekday\u{1f}A day of the week, `Monday` to"
    " `Sunday`.\u{1f}\u{1f}export enum Weekday\n    in `std::time`\n\n    A day of the week, `Monday` to `Sunday`.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}s"
    "td::time\u{1f}DateTime::from_unix\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn DateTime::from_unix(i64 s) : DateTime\u{1f}The moment `s` secon"
    "ds after 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z, or before it when `s` is negative.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn DateTime::from_unix(i64"
    " s) : DateTime\n    in `std::time`\n\n    The moment `s` seconds after 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z, or before "
    "it when `s` is negative.\n\n    Every `i64` names a moment, so this never fails.\n\n    Example, \"DateTi"
    "me::from_unix: before 1970\":\n        DateTime t = DateTime::from_unix(-1);\n        assert(t.year == "
    "1969 && t.month == 12 && t.day == 31);\n        assert(t.hour == 23 && t.minute == 59 && t.second == "
    "59);\n        assert(DateTime::to_unix(&t) == -1);\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::time\u{1f}DateTime::now\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn DateTime::n"
    "ow() : DateTime\u{1f}The moment now by the wall clock, to the second: `DateTime::from_unix(unix_seconds()"
    ")`.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn DateTime::now() : DateTime\n    in `std::time`\n\n    The moment now by the wall clock, "
    "to the second: `DateTime::from_unix(unix_seconds())`.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::time\u{1f}DateTime::new\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn DateTim"
    "e::new(i64 year, u8 month, u8 day, u8 hour, u8 minute, u8 second) : Option<DateTime>\u{1f}The `DateTime` "
    "with these fields, or `None` when they name no moment.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn DateTime::new(i64 year, u8 month, "
    "u8 day, u8 hour, u8 minute, u8 second) : Option<DateTime>\n    in `std::time`\n\n    The `DateTime` wit"
    "h these fields, or `None` when they name no moment.\n\n    `None` for a month 13, a 30 February, a 29 "
    "February in a common year, an hour 24 or a second 60. A leap\n    year is divisible by 4 and not by 1"
    "00, or by 400.\n\n    Example, \"DateTime::new: 29 February\":\n        Option<DateTime> leap = DateTime:"
    ":new(2024, 2, 29, 0, 0, 0);\n        Option<DateTime> common = DateTime::new(2023, 2, 29, 0, 0, 0);\n "
    "       assert(Option::is_some(&leap));\n        assert(Option::is_none(&common));\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::time\u{1f}DateTim"
    "e::to_unix\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn DateTime::to_unix(ref<DateTime, shared> t) : i64\u{1f}The seconds from 1970-01-01"
    "T00:00:00Z to the moment `t`, negative before it; the inverse of `from_unix`.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn DateTime::t"
    "o_unix(ref<DateTime, shared> t) : i64\n    in `std::time`\n    requires\n        DateTime::valid(t), in"
    "valid_datetime\n\n    The seconds from 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z to the moment `t`, negative before it; the"
    " inverse of `from_unix`.\n\n    Faults (`invalid_datetime`) when the fields of `t` name no moment, or "
    "when the count does not fit in an\n    `i64`.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::time\u{1f}DateTime::weekday\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn DateTime::we"
    "ekday(ref<DateTime, shared> t) : Weekday\u{1f}The day of the week of the date of `t`.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn DateTime"
    "::weekday(ref<DateTime, shared> t) : Weekday\n    in `std::time`\n\n    The day of the week of the date"
    " of `t`.\n\n    Defined for every valid `t`, however far from 1970. Faults (`invalid_datetime`) when t"
    "he date of `t`\n    names no day.\n\n    Example, \"DateTime::weekday: 1970-01-01\":\n        DateTime t ="
    " DateTime::from_unix(0);\n        Weekday w = DateTime::weekday(&t);\n        assert(Weekday::text(&w)"
    " == \"Thursday\");\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::time\u{1f}Weekday::text\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Weekday::text(ref<Weekday, shared> w) : str\u{1f}"
    "The day's English name (`Monday`).\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Weekday::text(ref<Weekday, shared> w) : str\n    in `st"
    "d::time`\n\n    The day's English name (`Monday`).\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::time\u{1f}DateTime::to_iso\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn DateTime:"
    ":to_iso(ref<DateTime, shared> t) : String\u{1f}The moment in ISO 8601 form in UTC, such as `2026-10-03T10"
    ":17:00Z`.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn DateTime::to_iso(ref<DateTime, shared> t) : String\n    in `std::time`\n\n    The "
    "moment in ISO 8601 form in UTC, such as `2026-10-03T10:17:00Z`.\n\n    Each field is zero-padded to it"
    "s width. A year outside 0 ..= 9999 is written with its sign and at least\n    four digits (`-0001`, `"
    "+10000`). Use `iso_from_unix_ms` for a timestamp with milliseconds.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::time\u{1f}DateTime::from_iso\u{1f}f"
    "n\u{1f}export fn DateTime::from_iso(StringView text) : Result<DateTime, ParseError>\u{1f}Reads a moment in `to"
    "_iso`'s form, or a date alone (`YYYY-MM-DD`) as its midnight.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn DateTime::from_iso(StringVi"
    "ew text) : Result<DateTime, ParseError>\n    in `std::time`\n\n    Reads a moment in `to_iso`'s form, o"
    "r a date alone (`YYYY-MM-DD`) as its midnight.\n\n    The year is four digits, or a sign and four or m"
    "ore. The seconds may carry a fraction (`:37.25Z`), which\n    is dropped; `unix_ms_from_iso` keeps it"
    " to the millisecond. No other form is read.\n\n    - `Err(Empty)` for the empty text.\n    - `Err(Inval"
    "id(i))`: byte `i` is the first that does not fit the form (the length when the text stops\n      shor"
    "t).\n    - `Err(OutOfRange)`: the fields name no moment (a month 13, a 29 February in a common year, "
    "a second\n      60), or the year is beyond `i64`.\n\n    Example, \"DateTime::from_iso: a date alone, a "
    "short time\":\n        match (DateTime::from_iso(\"2026-10-03\"))\n        {\n            Ok(t)  : assert("
    "t.day == 3 && t.hour == 0),\n            Err(_) : assert(false),\n        }\n        match (DateTime::f"
    "rom_iso(\"2026-10-03T10:17\"))\n        {\n            Err(Invalid(i)) : assert(i == 16),\n            _ "
    "              : assert(false),\n        }\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::time\u{1f}DateTime::eq\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn DateTime::eq(ref<Date"
    "Time, shared> a, ref<DateTime, shared> b) : bool\u{1f}Whether `a` and `b` are the same moment, every fiel"
    "d equal.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn DateTime::eq(ref<DateTime, shared> a, ref<DateTime, shared> b) : bool\n    in `st"
    "d::time`\n\n    Whether `a` and `b` are the same moment, every field equal.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::time\u{1f}DateTime::less"
    "\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn DateTime::less(ref<DateTime, shared> a, ref<DateTime, shared> b) : bool\u{1f}Whether `a` is"
    " earlier than `b`, comparing the fields in order of significance.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn DateTime::less(ref<Date"
    "Time, shared> a, ref<DateTime, shared> b) : bool\n    in `std::time`\n\n    Whether `a` is earlier than"
    " `b`, comparing the fields in order of significance.\n\n    For valid values this is the order of `to_"
    "unix`. It fits `Vec::sort_by`.\n\n    Example, \"DateTime::less: ordering\":\n        DateTime a = DateTi"
    "me::from_unix(100);\n        DateTime b = DateTime::from_unix(160);\n        assert(DateTime::less(&a,"
    " &b));\n        assert(!DateTime::less(&b, &a));\n        assert(!DateTime::eq(&a, &b));\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::time\u{1f}u"
    "nix_ms\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn unix_ms() : i64\u{1f}The wall clock in milliseconds since 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z, round"
    "ed down.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn unix_ms() : i64\n    in `std::time`\n\n    The wall clock in milliseconds since 197"
    "0-01-01T00:00:00Z, rounded down.\n\n    `unix_ms() / 1000` is `unix_seconds()` when read together. The"
    " wall clock may be changed while the\n    program runs; measure elapsed time with `monotonic_ns`.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}s"
    "td::time\u{1f}local_offset_seconds\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn local_offset_seconds(i64 unix) : i64\u{1f}How many seconds the"
    " machine's local time is ahead of UTC at the moment `unix` seconds after 1970.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn local_offs"
    "et_seconds(i64 unix) : i64\n    in `std::time`\n\n    How many seconds the machine's local time is ahea"
    "d of UTC at the moment `unix` seconds after 1970.\n\n    Negative when local time is behind UTC; dayli"
    "ght saving included, by the operating system's rule for the\n    zone the program runs in. Between -6"
    "4800 and 64800. For a moment the system has no rule for, the offset\n    now. It differs between mach"
    "ines, and on one machine between moments.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::time\u{1f}DateTime::to_local\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn DateTime::to_l"
    "ocal(ref<DateTime, shared> t) : DateTime\u{1f}The moment `t`, given in UTC, written in the machine's loca"
    "l time.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn DateTime::to_local(ref<DateTime, shared> t) : DateTime\n    in `std::time`\n\n    Th"
    "e moment `t`, given in UTC, written in the machine's local time.\n\n    The result is a `DateTime` lik"
    "e any other and knows nothing of its zone: print its fields, not `to_iso`\n    (which writes `Z`), an"
    "d keep the UTC value for arithmetic, since `to_unix` of the result is the moment\n    plus the offset"
    ". Faults (`invalid_datetime`) when `t` names no moment, or when the moment is within a\n    day of an"
    " `i64` limit.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::time\u{1f}monotonic_ns\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn monotonic_ns() : u64\u{1f}Nanoseconds on a monotonic "
    "clock, from a start fixed for the whole program.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn monotonic_ns() : u64\n    in `std::time`\n"
    "\n    Nanoseconds on a monotonic clock, from a start fixed for the whole program.\n\n    The clock neve"
    "r goes backwards, so the difference of two readings measures how long something took. Its\n    values"
    " mean nothing else; use `unix_ms` for the time of day.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::time\u{1f}unix_seconds\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn unix_se"
    "conds() : i64\u{1f}The wall clock: whole seconds since 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn unix_seconds() : "
    "i64\n    in `std::time`\n\n    The wall clock: whole seconds since 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z.\n\n    The wall "
    "clock may be changed while the program runs; measure elapsed time with `monotonic_ns`.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::time\u{1f}s"
    "leep_ms\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn sleep_ms(u64 ms)\u{1f}Makes the calling thread do nothing for at least `ms` millisec"
    "onds; other threads run meanwhile.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn sleep_ms(u64 ms)\n    in `std::time`\n\n    Makes the cal"
    "ling thread do nothing for at least `ms` milliseconds; other threads run meanwhile.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::time\u{1f}iso_"
    "from_unix_ms\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn iso_from_unix_ms(i64 ms) : String\u{1f}The moment `ms` milliseconds after 1970-"
    "01-01T00:00:00Z in `to_iso`'s form, with milliseconds.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn iso_from_unix_ms(i64 ms) : String\n"
    "    in `std::time`\n\n    The moment `ms` milliseconds after 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z in `to_iso`'s form, "
    "with milliseconds.\n\n    The form is `2026-10-07T12:34:56.789Z`, a log line's or a JSON document's ti"
    "mestamp. A negative `ms`\n    is before 1970, the milliseconds still counted forward within their sec"
    "ond.\n\n    Example, \"iso_from_unix_ms: before 1970\":\n        assert(iso_from_unix_ms(-1) == \"1969-12-"
    "31T23:59:59.999Z\");\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::time\u{1f}unix_ms_from_iso\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn unix_ms_from_iso(StringView text) : Re"
    "sult<i64, ParseError>\u{1f}The milliseconds since 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z of a moment in `DateTime::from_iso"
    "`'s form.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn unix_ms_from_iso(StringView text) : Result<i64, ParseError>\n    in `std::time`\n"
    "\n    The milliseconds since 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z of a moment in `DateTime::from_iso`'s form.\n\n    A "
    "fraction of the seconds is kept to the millisecond: its first three digits, fewer filled with zeros,"
    "\n    further digits ignored (`.5` is 500, `.1234` is 123). The errors are `from_iso`'s; `Err(OutOfRa"
    "nge)`\n    also when the milliseconds do not fit in an `i64`.\n\n    Example, \"unix_ms_from_iso: fracti"
    "ons\":\n        assert(unix_ms_from_iso(\"1970-01-01T00:00:01.5Z\") == Ok(1500));\n        assert(unix_ms"
    "_from_iso(\"1970-01-01T00:00:00.1239Z\") == Ok(123));\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::time\u{1f}DateTime::to_http_date\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn "
    "DateTime::to_http_date(ref<DateTime, shared> t) : String\u{1f}The moment in HTTP's date form, such as `Su"
    "n, 06 Nov 1994 08:49:37 GMT`.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn DateTime::to_http_date(ref<DateTime, shared> t) : String\n  "
    "  in `std::time`\n    requires\n        t.year >= 0 && t.year <= 9999, invalid_datetime\n\n    The momen"
    "t in HTTP's date form, such as `Sun, 06 Nov 1994 08:49:37 GMT`.\n\n    The form (RFC 9110 §5.6.7, the"
    " IMF-fixdate) is what the `Date`, `Last-Modified` and `Expires` headers\n    carry. Faults (`invalid_"
    "datetime`) for a year outside 0 ..= 9999, or fields that name no moment.\n\n    Example, \"DateTime::to"
    "_http_date: the IMF-fixdate\":\n        DateTime t = DateTime::from_unix(784111777);\n        assert(Da"
    "teTime::to_http_date(&t) == \"Sun, 06 Nov 1994 08:49:37 GMT\");\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::time\u{1f}DateTime::from_http_date\u{1f}f"
    "n\u{1f}export fn DateTime::from_http_date(StringView text) : Result<DateTime, ParseError>\u{1f}Reads an HTTP d"
    "ate in any of the three forms a recipient must accept (RFC 9110 §5.6.7).\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn DateTime::from_"
    "http_date(StringView text) : Result<DateTime, ParseError>\n    in `std::time`\n\n    Reads an HTTP date"
    " in any of the three forms a recipient must accept (RFC 9110 §5.6.7).\n\n    The forms are `Sun, 06 N"
    "ov 1994 08:49:37 GMT` (the one to send), the obsolete RFC 850\n    `Sunday, 06-Nov-94 08:49:37 GMT` ("
    "a two-digit year: 70 to 99 are 1970 to 1999, 00 to 69 are 2000 to\n    2069) and asctime's `Sun Nov  "
    "6 08:49:37 1994`. The words are separated by ASCII whitespace; the weekday\n    is not checked agains"
    "t the date.\n\n    - `Err(Empty)` when the text has no words.\n    - `Err(Invalid(i))`: `i` is the star"
    "t of the first word that does not fit.\n    - `Err(OutOfRange)`: the fields name no moment.\n\n    Exam"
    "ple, \"DateTime::from_http_date: a two-digit year\":\n        match (DateTime::from_http_date(\"Sunday, "
    "06-Nov-94 08:49:37 GMT\"))\n        {\n            Ok(t)  : assert(t.year == 1994 && t.month == 11 && t"
    ".day == 6),\n            Err(_) : assert(false),\n        }\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::process\u{1f}Command\u{1f}struct\u{1f}export struc"
    "t Command\u{1f}A program to run: its name or path, its arguments, its working directory and its environme"
    "nt.\u{1f}\u{1f}export struct Command\n    in `std::process`\n\n    A program to run: its name or path, its argume"
    "nts, its working directory and its environment.\n\n    Made by `Command::new`, changed by `arg`, `curr"
    "ent_dir`, `env` and `clear_env`, and run by `output`,\n    `output_with_input`, `status` or `spawn`, "
    "any number of times. No shell is involved: each argument\n    reaches the program exactly as given, w"
    "ith no splitting, quoting or expansion; a program that wants a\n    shell runs one by name (`/bin/sh`"
    " with `-c`).\n\n    Running fails with `Err(NotFound)` when there is no such program, `Err(Denied)` wh"
    "en it may not be run,\n    and `Err(Io)` for any other failure to start it (a working directory that "
    "does not exist among them).\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::process\u{1f}Output\u{1f}struct\u{1f}export struct Output\u{1f}How a run of `Command:"
    ":output` ended, and everything the program wrote.\u{1f}\u{1f}export struct Output\n    in `std::process`\n\n    H"
    "ow a run of `Command::output` ended, and everything the program wrote.\n\n    - `status`: the exit cod"
    "e, or on Unix minus the number of the signal that ended it (`-9` for SIGKILL).\n    - `stdout`: every"
    " byte the program wrote to its standard output.\n    - `stderr`: every byte the program wrote to its "
    "standard error.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::process\u{1f}Command::new\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Command::new(StringView program) : Command\u{1f}"
    "A command running `program` with no arguments, in this program's working directory and environment.\u{1f}"
    "\u{1f}export fn Command::new(StringView program) : Command\n    in `std::process`\n\n    A command running `"
    "program` with no arguments, in this program's working directory and environment.\n\n    A name without"
    " a separator is looked for on `PATH`, as the system does.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::process\u{1f}Command::arg\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn C"
    "ommand::arg(ref<Command, exclusive> c, StringView a)\u{1f}Appends one argument, passed to the program exa"
    "ctly as given.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Command::arg(ref<Command, exclusive> c, StringView a)\n    in `std::process"
    "`\n\n    Appends one argument, passed to the program exactly as given.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::process\u{1f}Command::current"
    "_dir\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Command::current_dir(ref<Command, exclusive> c, StringView dir)\u{1f}Sets the working d"
    "irectory the program starts in.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Command::current_dir(ref<Command, exclusive> c, StringVie"
    "w dir)\n    in `std::process`\n\n    Sets the working directory the program starts in.\n\n    A directory"
    " that does not exist makes the run fail with `Err(Io)`.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::process\u{1f}Command::env\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Com"
    "mand::env(ref<Command, exclusive> c, StringView name, StringView value)\u{1f}Sets the environment variabl"
    "e `name` to `value` for the program.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Command::env(ref<Command, exclusive> c, StringView n"
    "ame, StringView value)\n    in `std::process`\n\n    Sets the environment variable `name` to `value` fo"
    "r the program.\n\n    The variables set this way go on top of the inherited environment, or of an empt"
    "y one after\n    `Command::clear_env`.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::process\u{1f}Command::clear_env\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Command::clear_"
    "env(ref<Command, exclusive> c)\u{1f}Makes the program start with no environment variables but those `Comm"
    "and::env` sets.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Command::clear_env(ref<Command, exclusive> c)\n    in `std::process`\n\n    "
    "Makes the program start with no environment variables but those `Command::env` sets.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::process\u{1f}"
    "Command::output\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Command::output(ref<Command, shared> c) : Result<Output, FileError>\u{1f}Run"
    "s the program to its end with an empty standard input, and gives its status and everything it wrote."
    "\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Command::output(ref<Command, shared> c) : Result<Output, FileError>\n    in `std::process"
    "`\n\n    Runs the program to its end with an empty standard input, and gives its status and everything"
    " it wrote.\n\n    Both streams are read at once, so neither pipe fills and stops the program.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::p"
    "rocess\u{1f}Command::output_with_input\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Command::output_with_input(ref<Command, shared> c, sl"
    "ice<u8, shared> input) : Result<Output, FileError>\u{1f}Runs the program to its end with `input` as its s"
    "tandard input, as `Command::output` does.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Command::output_with_input(ref<Command, shared>"
    " c, slice<u8, shared> input) : Result<Output, FileError>\n    in `std::process`\n\n    Runs the program"
    " to its end with `input` as its standard input, as `Command::output` does.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::process\u{1f}Command::s"
    "tatus\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Command::status(ref<Command, shared> c) : Result<i32, FileError>\u{1f}Runs the program"
    " to its end with this program's standard input, output and error, and gives its status.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn C"
    "ommand::status(ref<Command, shared> c) : Result<i32, FileError>\n    in `std::process`\n\n    Runs the "
    "program to its end with this program's standard input, output and error, and gives its status.\n\n    "
    "The status is `Output::status`'s: the exit code, or on Unix minus the signal that ended it.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::p"
    "rocess\u{1f}Child\u{1f}struct\u{1f}export resource struct Child\u{1f}A running child process, its standard input, output"
    " and error each a pipe to this program.\u{1f}\u{1f}export resource struct Child\n    in `std::process`\n\n    A r"
    "unning child process, its standard input, output and error each a pipe to this program.\n\n    Made by"
    " `Command::spawn`. Destroying a `Child` closes its pipes but does not end it: a running child\n    ru"
    "ns on. A child that fills a pipe nobody reads stops until it is read. `Child::take_input` and\n    `C"
    "hild::take_output` move its pipes out, for use from other threads.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::process\u{1f}Command::spawn\u{1f}fn\u{1f}"
    "export fn Command::spawn(ref<Command, shared> c) : Result<Child, FileError>\u{1f}Starts the program and r"
    "eturns at once, its three streams pipes to this program.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Command::spawn(ref<Command, shar"
    "ed> c) : Result<Child, FileError>\n    in `std::process`\n\n    Starts the program and returns at once,"
    " its three streams pipes to this program.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::process\u{1f}Child::id\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Child::id(ref<Child,"
    " shared> ch) : u64\u{1f}The operating system's number for the child (its process id).\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Child::i"
    "d(ref<Child, shared> ch) : u64\n    in `std::process`\n\n    The operating system's number for the chil"
    "d (its process id).\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::process\u{1f}Child::write_input\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Child::write_input(ref<Child, exc"
    "lusive> ch, slice<u8, shared> data) : Result<void, FileError>\u{1f}Writes all of `data` to the child's st"
    "andard input.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Child::write_input(ref<Child, exclusive> ch, slice<u8, shared> data) : Resu"
    "lt<void, FileError>\n    in `std::process`\n\n    Writes all of `data` to the child's standard input.\n\n"
    "    `Err(Io)` when the child has ended or closed its input; it never ends this program. Does nothing"
    " once\n    `Child::take_input` has taken the input.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::process\u{1f}Child::close_input\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Ch"
    "ild::close_input(ref<Child, exclusive> ch)\u{1f}Closes the child's standard input, so that the child read"
    "s its end.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Child::close_input(ref<Child, exclusive> ch)\n    in `std::process`\n\n    Closes"
    " the child's standard input, so that the child reads its end.\n\n    Does nothing once `Child::take_in"
    "put` has taken the input.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::process\u{1f}Child::read_output\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Child::read_output(ref<Chil"
    "d, exclusive> ch, ref<Vec<u8>, exclusive> buf, usize max) : Result<usize, FileError>\u{1f}Appends to `buf"
    "` what has arrived on the child's standard output, up to `max` bytes, and returns how many.\u{1f}\u{1f}export "
    "fn Child::read_output(ref<Child, exclusive> ch, ref<Vec<u8>, exclusive> buf, usize max) : Result<usi"
    "ze, FileError>\n    in `std::process`\n\n    Appends to `buf` what has arrived on the child's standard "
    "output, up to `max` bytes, and returns how many.\n\n    Waits for at least one byte; at most a MiB is "
    "read at a time. `Ok(0)` (for a `max` above 0) means the\n    end of the output.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::process\u{1f}Child:"
    ":read_output_line\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Child::read_output_line(ref<Child, exclusive> ch) : Result<Option<Str"
    "ing>, FileError>\u{1f}The next line of the child's standard output, without its `'\\n'` or a `'\\r'` just b"
    "efore it.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Child::read_output_line(ref<Child, exclusive> ch) : Result<Option<String>, File"
    "Error>\n    in `std::process`\n\n    The next line of the child's standard output, without its `'\\n'` o"
    "r a `'\\r'` just before it.\n\n    `Ok(None)` at the end of the output; `Err(Utf8(e))` for a line that "
    "is not UTF-8.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::process\u{1f}Child::read_error\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Child::read_error(ref<Child, exclusive> "
    "ch, ref<Vec<u8>, exclusive> buf, usize max) : Result<usize, FileError>\u{1f}Appends to `buf` what has arr"
    "ived on the child's standard error, as `Child::read_output` does.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Child::read_error(ref<C"
    "hild, exclusive> ch, ref<Vec<u8>, exclusive> buf, usize max) : Result<usize, FileError>\n    in `std:"
    ":process`\n\n    Appends to `buf` what has arrived on the child's standard error, as `Child::read_outp"
    "ut` does.\n\n    What `Child::read_error_line` has read ahead comes first.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::process\u{1f}Child::read_"
    "error_line\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Child::read_error_line(ref<Child, exclusive> ch) : Result<Option<String>, Fi"
    "leError>\u{1f}The next line of the child's standard error, as `Child::read_output_line` reads standard ou"
    "tput.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Child::read_error_line(ref<Child, exclusive> ch) : Result<Option<String>, FileError"
    ">\n    in `std::process`\n\n    The next line of the child's standard error, as `Child::read_output_lin"
    "e` reads standard output.\n\n    Each stream is read ahead on its own.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::process\u{1f}Child::wait\u{1f}fn\u{1f}e"
    "xport fn Child::wait(ref<Child, exclusive> ch) : Result<i32, FileError>\u{1f}Closes the child's standard "
    "input, waits for the child to end and gives its status.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Child::wait(ref<Child, exclusive>"
    " ch) : Result<i32, FileError>\n    in `std::process`\n\n    Closes the child's standard input, waits fo"
    "r the child to end and gives its status.\n\n    The status is `Output::status`'s. Asked again, it give"
    "s the same status.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::process\u{1f}Child::try_wait\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Child::try_wait(ref<Child, exclusive>"
    " ch) : Result<Option<i32>, FileError>\u{1f}The child's status if it has ended, without waiting.\u{1f}\u{1f}export f"
    "n Child::try_wait(ref<Child, exclusive> ch) : Result<Option<i32>, FileError>\n    in `std::process`\n\n"
    "    The child's status if it has ended, without waiting.\n\n    `Ok(None)` while it runs.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::proce"
    "ss\u{1f}Child::kill\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Child::kill(ref<Child, exclusive> ch) : Result<void, FileError>\u{1f}Ends the"
    " child at once (SIGKILL on Unix, TerminateProcess on Windows).\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Child::kill(ref<Child, exc"
    "lusive> ch) : Result<void, FileError>\n    in `std::process`\n\n    Ends the child at once (SIGKILL on "
    "Unix, TerminateProcess on Windows).\n\n    `Child::wait` then gives its status. Nothing happens to a c"
    "hild that has already ended. Use\n    `Child::terminate` to let the child stop cleanly, and `Child::k"
    "ill_tree` to end the processes it started\n    too.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::process\u{1f}Child::kill_tree\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Chil"
    "d::kill_tree(ref<Child, exclusive> ch) : Result<void, FileError>\u{1f}Ends the child and every process it"
    " started, and theirs in turn.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Child::kill_tree(ref<Child, exclusive> ch) : Result<void, F"
    "ileError>\n    in `std::process`\n\n    Ends the child and every process it started, and theirs in turn"
    ".\n\n    On Linux the processes are found from `/proc`; on Windows by `taskkill /T`; elsewhere only th"
    "e child is\n    ended, as `Child::kill` ends it. `Child::wait` then gives its status.\n\u{1f}\u{1e}std::process\u{1f}"
    "Child::terminate\u{1f}fn\u{1f}export fn Child::terminate(ref<Child, exclusive> ch) : Result<void, FileError>\u{1f}A"
    "sks the child to end: SIGTERM on Unix, which a program may catch to stop cleanly.\u{1f}\u{1f}export fn Child::"
    "terminate(ref<Child, exclusive> ch) : Result<void, FileError>\n    in `std::process`\n\n    Asks the ch"
    "ild to end: SIGTERM on Unix, which a program may catch to stop cleanly.\n\n    On Windows it ends the "
    "child as `Child::kill` does. On Unix the status `Child::wait` then gives is -15,\n    unless the chil"
    "d handles the signal. Nothing happens, and `Ok(())` is returned, for a child that has\n    already en"
